// Chapter 12, "Checkride." The last chapter. Sources read for this chapter (verified 2026-10-11):
// Private Pilot for Airplane Category ACS, FAA-S-ACS-6C: https://www.faa.gov/training_testing/testing/acs/private_airplane_acs_6.pdf
//   Appendix 1 (oral throughout the test, scenario and correlation questions; minimum elements per task: every element missed on the knowledge
//   test, at least one K, at least one R, all skills; the ground portion must be passed before the flight; the evaluator builds a plan of
//   action with scenarios; three outcomes: temporary certificate, Notice of Disapproval of Application, Letter of Discontinuance; a task may be
//   repeated if incomplete or uncertain, but no instruction or practice; failing a task fails the area; the test continues only with the
//   applicant's consent; credit only for areas passed; letter of discontinuance for weather, equipment, illness). Appendix 2 (positive
//   exchange of flight controls, three steps; distractions; failure to use ADM fails the task). Appendix 3 (view limiting device, 61.45(d)(2)).
//   Tolerances used: III.B.S5 pattern altitude +/-100 ft, airspeed +/-10 kt; IV.A, IV.C, IV.E climb speeds +10/-5; IV.B.S10 normal landing within
//   400 ft beyond the point; IV.F.S7 approach speed +10/-5 with gust factor, IV.F.S10 short field touchdown within 200 ft beyond or on the
//   point, minimum float; IV.M.S8 forward slip within 400 ft; V.A.S5 steep turns altitude +/-100, airspeed +/-10, bank +/-5 (about 45), rollout
//   heading +/-10; V.B.S7 +/-100, +/-10; VI.A to VI.C altitude +/-200, heading +/-15; VII.A.S5 slow flight altitude +/-100, heading +/-10,
//   airspeed +10/-0, bank +/-10; VII.B and C heading +/-10, bank not over 20 +/-10; VIII.A to D altitude +/-200, heading +/-20 (turns: rollout
//   +/-10), airspeed +/-10; IX.A.S5 +0/-10 and +/-100; IX.B.S1 best glide +/-10. II.A K1 to K4, R1 to R5, S1 to S4. II.B.K1, S2 (passenger
//   briefing: PIC, belts, harnesses, doors, conduct, sterile aircraft, propeller avoidance, emergency procedures). VI.C.S1 to S5. XII.A K1, K2,
//   R1, R3, R4, S2 to S5. The ACS task list for ASEL read from its table of contents. Note: the 6C normal landing standard is 400 feet, not 200.
// 14 CFR, eCFR versioner API, Title 14 as of 2026-10-01: https://www.ecfr.gov/api/versioner/v1/full/2026-10-01/title-14.xml?part=61&section=61.43
//   (same call for part=61 sections 61.17, 61.39, 61.45, 61.47, 61.49, 61.56, 61.57, 61.87, 61.93, 61.103, 61.109; part=91 sections 91.107
//   and 91.159). 61.17(a) temporary certificate up to 120 days. 61.39(a)(1) to (7). 61.43(a), (c) to (f) (fail any area, fail the test;
//   discontinue for failure or for weather, airworthiness, or safety; credit for areas passed if the rest is passed within 60 days, with the
//   original notice or letter, any training and endorsements, and a new signed application). 61.45(d)(2). 61.47(a) to (c) (examiner is not PIC
//   unless agreed; passenger rules don't apply on the test). 61.49(a). 61.56(c), (d)(1). 61.57(a)(1). 61.93(c)(3) (endorsement for each solo
//   cross-country). 61.103(d) to (j). 61.109(a)(4), (a)(5). 91.107(a)(1) to (3). 91.159(a).
// AC 61-65K (11/14/25): https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Advisory_Circular/AC_61-65K.pdf . A.1 (61.39(a)(6)(i) and (ii)), A.2
//   (61.39(a)(6)(iii)), A.37 (61.103(f), 61.107(b), 61.109; required in addition to A.1 and A.2), A.77 (61.49 retest; instructor also signs the
//   recommendation on the 8710-1 for each retake), paragraph 14.2 (retest after a notice of disapproval: new endorsement, instructor
//   recommendation, credit within 60 calendar days) and 14.3 (no new endorsement after a letter of discontinuance; 60 days or the whole test).
// IACRA Recommending Instructor manual: https://iacra.faa.gov/iacra_manuals/IACRA_Instruction_Manual_Recomending_Instructor.pdf (the
//   instructor retrieves, reviews, and signs the applicant's 8710-1 in IACRA).
// AIM 5-6-13 and 5-6-14, interception procedures and signals (follow me, you may proceed, land here; squawk 7700 unless told otherwise; call on
//   guard): https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap5_section_6.html . AIM 2-3 runway markings (aiming point about
//   1,000 feet from the threshold; touchdown zone bars in 500 foot steps): https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap2_section_3.html
// PHAK, FAA-H-8083-25C: https://www.faa.gov/regulations_policies/handbooks_manuals/aviation/faa-h-8083-25c.pdf (frost disrupts airflow, cuts lift,
//   adds drag; an aircraft must be thoroughly cleaned and free of frost before flight; 0.8 mm of ice on the upper wing can cut lift 25 percent).
// AirNav, FAA information effective 01 October 2026: https://www.airnav.com/airport/KAKO (Colorado Plains Regional, FAA identifier AKO,
//   40-10-32.3000N 103-13-19.3000W, 4,716 ft surveyed, 1 mile N of Akron, no tower, CTAF, attended 0800 to 1700, beacon, runway 11/29 7,001 x 100
//   asphalt, left traffic both ways, PAPI both ends, 24 hour self-serve 100LL by card, FBO with courtesy transportation, crop spraying April to
//   October) and https://www.airnav.com/airport/KAPA (39-34-12.4270N 104-50-57.4550W, 5,884.9 ft, 17L/35R 10,001 x 100, 35R right traffic).
//   No restaurant is listed at the field, so the diner is in town, on Main Avenue (Main Avenue is the town's address on the AirNav record), and
//   it has no name on purpose.
// Route, by haversine from AirNav coordinates: KAPA to KAKO direct 83.3 NM at 064 true. Flown as in chapter 9, KAPA to BEND (39-37.2N
//   104-30.0W) 16.4 NM at 079, then BEND to KAKO 67.7 NM at 060 true, 84.1 total. Against the Denver Class B text chapter 9 cites (FR 2026-06762):
//   BEND sits 13.7 NM from the point of origin under the 8,000 floor; the leg east passes 10 to 15 NM out only south of the 090 radial, then
//   20 to 30 NM out between the 078 and 096 radials (floor 10,000), and leaves the Class B 27 NM past BEND. Magnetic course with 7 E (chapter 9's
//   NOAA figure, rounded) 053, so odd thousands plus 500; 9,500 is more than 3,000 AGL there. Diversion point 14 NM past BEND (20.1 NM from the
//   point of origin): to KCFO 14.2 NM at 282 true, 275 magnetic. KCFO to KAPA 19.3 at 228.
// Dates: 2027 daylight saving time begins Sunday, March 14 (the second Sunday), so March 19 onward is MDT, UTC minus 6. Weekdays checked by
//   calendar. Shifts continue every 3 days from chapter 11: Feb 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21, 24, 27, Mar 2, 5, 8, 11, 14, 17, 20, 23, 26, 29, Apr 1.
//   March 21, 2027 is a Sunday; the chapter treats it as the anniversary (Danny died on a Saturday in March 2026; March 21, 2026 was a Saturday).
//   March 19 + 60 days = May 18. March 22 + 60 = May 21. March 22 + 120 = July 20. March 25 + 120 = July 23.
// Invented for the story: Kendra Lowe and everything she does; her fee (no amount on purpose); the regional airline and its class dates; every
//   METAR and TAF (written in format, times before the story clock); the frost; the diner; all weights after chapter 5 (Ellie 79 lb in March);
//   every Hobbs time after chapter 11 (solo Feb 11 1.2 and Feb 23 1.1; prep Feb 17 1.3, Mar 4 1.2, Mar 10 1.1, Mar 16 1.0; checkride 2.1,
//   retest 0.6); the letter of discontinuance, notice of disapproval, and temporary certificate layouts beyond the items the ACS and 61.43 name.
//   No frequencies appear on purpose.
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).pilot=window.HALCYON_DATA.pilot||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.pilot.full||[]).push({
  num: 12, part: `Part Two: The Trip`, title: `Checkride`, weeks: `February to April`,
  tasks: [`II`,`XII`,`I.A`,`IV`,`I.C`,`I.F`,`III.A`,`VI`],
  cast: [
    [`Kendra Lowe`,`Designated pilot examiner. Calm, exacting, and in no hurry.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `On the Friday after the written, Abby put a chair in the middle of the briefing room, sat Jordan in it, stood behind the whiteboard with her arms folded, and said she was an F-16 and she was annoyed. Jordan had to explain what Biscuit would do if the F-16 rocked its wings and turned slowly left. She did. Abby rocked the whiteboard eraser back at her, which was apparently the fighter saying thanks.`,
    `The chart legend took two evenings at the club table with a highlighter and the reading glasses she claimed she only needed for medication labels. NTSB 830 took one, out loud, as a lawyer, with Abby playing the hysterical passenger holding up a finger that was absolutely, definitely, broken. A simple fracture of a finger is not a serious injury. Jordan said it eleven times. On the twelfth she said it like she believed it, and Abby opened the logbook to the endorsement page.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Jordan's logbook, endorsements">01/21/27. I certify that Jordan E. Reyes has demonstrated satisfactory knowledge of the subject areas in which they were deficient on the Private Pilot Airplane airman knowledge test.<br>/s/ 01/21/27 A. Strand 3418862CFI RE 06-30-2027</prop>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"That's A.2. A.1 and A.37 come at the end, after the prep. The prep has to be inside the two calendar months before the month you test, so we don't start it until we know the month. Which means Walt."</span>`,
    `Walt had known every examiner on the Front Range for thirty years, and he had opinions about all of them, most of which he'd keep to himself until you bought him a breakfast burrito. Jordan bought him two at the counter by the FBO on a Tuesday in January. He ate one, wrapped the other in a napkin for later, and wrote a name on the napkin.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Kendra Lowe. Flew freight for twenty two years, night, in airplanes older than Biscuit. She asks you what you'd do, and then she asks why, and then she waits. You'll want to fill the quiet. Don't. She's fair. She isn't fast."</span> He folded the napkin over the name. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"I'll call her."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"I can call her."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"You can. I'm going to."</span>`,
    `He did. It took him a couple of weeks, because Kendra booked out like a dentist, and Walt didn't text Jordan until he had a date he liked. It came in on a Sunday morning, February 7, while she was standing in the bay at the end of a twenty four with a mop in her hand.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Walt Herrera" time="7:12 AM">Kendra Lowe. Friday March 19. 0730 at the FBO briefing room. After that she's full into May. Don't make me call her back.</prop>`,
    `No question mark. Jordan read it twice, the way she read everything from Walt, and then she did the math she'd been not doing since September. The year ran out in the second week of April. March 19 left a little under four weeks of margin for weather, a broken airplane, or a bad day. It was enough. It was not a lot.`,
    `She propped the mop against the wall and looked at the calendar on her phone, at March, and at the twenty first, that she'd been not looking at since the first of the year.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Abby's Ground School: the checkride, and everything after it`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Last one`,
        body: [`This is the last one I write for you. I'm going to try not to make it weird, and I'm going to fail, so let's get the weird part done. You were the student I was going to log and forget. You read every line of every page I ever handed you, and you made me read them again. That's the whole speech. Now, the practical test.`,
          `61.43(a) says what passing means: you perform the tasks in the areas of operation in the ACS for the certificate you want, you show mastery of the airplane, you stay within the standards, and you show sound judgment. That last one is in the regulation. Judgment is a graded item, and people forget that.`,
          `There's a ground portion and a flight portion, and you have to pass the ground part before Kendra will start the flight part. The questions don't stop when you leave the building, though. The ACS says the oral continues through the whole test, so expect questions at the runup, in the practice area, and on short final when she knows you're busy.`,
          `Under 61.47 the examiner represents the Administrator for the test. She is not the pilot in command unless the two of you agree on that ahead of time, and you won't. You are. You're a student pilot acting as pilot in command with a stranger in the right seat, which 61.47(c) allows, because the passenger rules don't apply to the people on a practical test. Act like it from the moment you meet her. If you'd say no to a friend, say no to Kendra.`],
        terms: [[`61.43(a)`,`Passing: the ACS tasks, mastery of the aircraft, performance within standards, and sound judgment.`],[`61.47(b)`,`The examiner isn't PIC unless that's agreed in advance. The applicant is.`],[`61.47(c)`,`Passenger carrying limits don't apply to the applicant and examiner on the test.`]],
        exam: `Expect a question about who is pilot in command during a practical test. Unless they've agreed otherwise, it's the applicant.` },
      { h: `What goes in the bag`,
        body: [`61.39 is the list of what has to be true before you're even eligible to take the test. Passed the knowledge test within the 24 calendar months before the month you finish the practical. The original report, with you, at the time of application. The training and the experience for the certificate. At least a third class medical. Old enough. The instructor endorsement, signed by me, saying you've had training inside the 2 calendar months before the month you apply, that you're prepared, and that you've shown me you know the areas you missed on the written. A completed and signed application.`,
          `The application is FAA Form 8710-1, and you'll fill it out in IACRA with the same FAA Tracking Number you used in May and at PSI. You type your times in from your logbook. Then I retrieve it, review it, and sign it as your recommending instructor, and Kendra pulls it up on her laptop the morning of the test. If anybody finds an error after I've signed, it goes back to you to fix, and I sign it again. Check your numbers before you submit, because Kendra will check them after.`,
          `In your logbook she'll want A.1, which is 61.39(a)(6)(i) and (ii), the training in the 2 calendar months and prepared for the test. A.2, which you already have, for the missed areas. And A.37, the flight proficiency endorsement under 61.103(f) and 61.109, which the AC says is required on top of A.1 and A.2. She'll also look at your solo endorsements and your 61.109 times line by line, because 61.39(a)(3) makes her verify the experience before she starts. Bring the student pilot certificate, your medical, a photo ID, and the knowledge test report with the raised seal.`,
          `Then the airplane. 61.45 says you furnish it, with the equipment for every area of operation and controls she can reach. She'll look at the airworthiness certificate and registration in the sleeve, the POH, the weight and balance, and the maintenance records: the annual, the ELT, the transponder check, and the ADs, including the selector one Marisol did in January and its recurring line. Bring the hood, because 61.45(d)(2) makes you supply the view limiting device for the instrument tasks. Bring a working pen. And pay her fee before she opens the oral. Examiners set their own, so ask when you book and don't be surprised. Walt has already told you the number with the face he uses for weather.`],
        terms: [[`61.39`,`Practical test prerequisites: knowledge test, AKTR, experience, medical, age, instructor endorsement, signed application.`],[`FAA Form 8710-1`,`The airman certificate application, filled out in IACRA, reviewed and signed by the recommending instructor.`],[`A.1, A.2, A.37`,`AC 61-65K endorsements: test prerequisites, missed-area review, and flight proficiency for the practical test.`],[`61.45(d)(2)`,`The applicant supplies a view limiting device for tasks flown by reference to instruments.`]],
        exam: `The written likes 61.39. Know the 24 calendar months for the knowledge test and the 2 calendar months for the instructor's prep endorsement.` },
      { h: `The oral`,
        body: [`Kendra will build what the ACS calls a plan of action. It covers every required area and task, and it's built around scenarios, because the ACS tells her to test whether you can apply and correlate what you know instead of reciting it. She won't ask you what the floor of the shelf is. She'll ask you to plan a trip under it with two passengers and a kid's seat cushion, and then she'll change the weather.`,
          `For every task she has to test at least one knowledge element and at least one risk management element, and every element you missed on the written. That's PA.I.D.K6, PA.I.E.K2, and PA.III.A.K8 for you. You will get asked about intercepts, the chart legend, and the NTSB. I promise. I'd bet my seniority number on it, if I had one yet.`,
          `Some answers you won't know cold. The good answer to some questions is "I'd look that up, and here's where," and then you open the AIM or the chart supplement or the POH and find it while she watches. Examiners like that answer. They don't like a guess wearing a confident face. And when she asks a question and you've answered it, stop talking. Walt's right about that. The quiet is hers. Let her have it.`],
        terms: [[`Plan of action`,`The examiner's plan covering every required area and task, built around scenarios.`],[`Correlation`,`Applying knowledge to a situation, as opposed to reciting it. The ACS prefers it.`],[`Missed elements`,`Every element on the AKTR must be tested on the practical test.`]],
        exam: `The ACS requires the evaluator to test the elements listed on the applicant's knowledge test report.` },
      { h: `The flight, and the numbers`,
        body: [`For a private certificate in a single engine land airplane you'll see every area that applies. Preflight procedures: the preflight, the flight deck and the passenger briefing, the engine start, taxi, and the runup. Communications and the pattern. Takeoffs and landings, all of them: normal, soft field, short field, the forward slip, and a go-around. Steep turns and one ground reference maneuver she chooses. Pilotage, the navigation systems, a diversion, and lost procedures. Slow flight, power off and power on stalls, and spin awareness, which is a conversation. The basic instrument tasks under the hood. An emergency descent, a simulated engine failure to a field, systems malfunctions, and survival gear. Night operations as knowledge. And postflight.`,
          `Here are the numbers that matter, straight out of FAA-S-ACS-6C. Learn them, then fly better than them. Pattern altitude plus or minus 100 feet and airspeed plus or minus 10 knots. Climb speeds plus 10, minus 5. Steep turns: about 45 degrees of bank, plus or minus 5, hold altitude within 100 feet and airspeed within 10 knots, and roll out within 10 degrees of the heading you started on. Slow flight: altitude within 100 feet, heading within 10 degrees, bank within 10 degrees, and airspeed plus 10, minus zero. Zero. You can be a little fast. You can't be slow. On the instrument tasks, 200 feet, 20 degrees, and 10 knots. Navigation and the diversion, 200 feet and 15 degrees. Best glide plus or minus 10.`,
          `Landings. On a normal landing you touch down within 400 feet beyond the point she picks, or on it. The commercial standard is 200, and people mix the two up. The forward slip to a landing is also 400. The short field is the tight one: on the point or within 200 feet beyond it, at the right pitch attitude, with minimum float, no side drift, and the airplane lined up on the centerline. Approach speed for both plus 10, minus 5, with the gust factor added in.`,
          `If she can't tell whether you did a task, or you didn't finish it, she can have you do it again. That's allowed. What isn't allowed during the test is practice or instruction. And going around is a maneuver, not an apology. A go-around flown well is something you'll be graded on. A landing forced onto a point you were never going to make is an action she has to correct, and the ACS lists that first among the ways to fail.`],
        terms: [[`Steep turns (V.A)`,`45° ±5° bank, ±100 ft, ±10 kt, rollout ±10°.`],[`Slow flight (VII.A)`,`±100 ft, ±10° heading, ±10° bank, airspeed +10/−0.`],[`Normal landing (IV.B)`,`Within 400 ft beyond or on the specified point.`],[`Short field landing (IV.F)`,`Within 200 ft beyond or on the point, minimum float, approach speed +10/−5.`]],
        exam: `Tolerances are mostly checkride material, but the reasons behind them show up on the written: why slow flight can't be slower than the target, why a short field approach is flown at an exact speed.` },
      { h: `Three ways it ends`,
        body: [`The ACS says a practical test has three possible outcomes. A temporary airman certificate, which means you passed. A Notice of Disapproval of Application, which means you didn't meet the standard on at least one task. Or a Letter of Discontinuance, which means the test stopped for some reason other than how you flew.`,
          `Fail one task and you fail that area of operation, and under 61.43(c) failing any area means you fail the test. She can stop there, or you can stop there. She can keep going only if you agree, and you probably should, because 61.43(f) gives you credit for the areas you passed if you pass the rest within 60 days. To come back you need the original notice, a new signed application, and more training from an instructor with an endorsement under 61.49 saying you're proficient. In AC 61-65K that's A.77, and I also have to sign the recommendation on the new application. Then you fly what's left. Lots of good pilots have a notice of disapproval in their file. It's a piece of paper with a date on it.`,
          `A Letter of Discontinuance is for weather, a broken airplane, illness, or anything else that makes stopping the safe call. 61.43(e)(2) covers that. The letter lists the areas you finished and how long you have to finish the rest. Same 60 days. No new endorsement needed, just the letter and your signed application. If the clock runs past 60 days, you start over.`],
        terms: [[`Notice of Disapproval`,`Issued when a task isn't performed to standard. Retest needs training, a 61.49 endorsement (A.77), and a new application.`],[`Letter of Discontinuance`,`Issued when the test stops for weather, airworthiness, illness, or safety. Lists completed areas.`],[`61.43(f)`,`Credit for areas passed if the rest is passed within 60 days.`]],
        exam: `Know the 60 days, and know which paper needs a new instructor endorsement. The notice does. The letter doesn't.` },
      { h: `Postflight, with an examiner watching`,
        body: [`Area XII again. You'll be tired and happy or tired and not, and either way the test isn't over until the airplane is secured. Park somewhere sensible, considering the people and airplanes around you. Run the checklist. Listen on the emergency frequency before shutdown. Mixture, mags, master. Control lock, chocks, ropes, pitot cover. Postflight walkaround. Write down anything you found, in words the next pilot can use.`,
          `The ACS calls out a risk I didn't stress enough in December: people moving around on the ramp. Passengers getting out, and people walking up to the airplane. When the propeller is turning, nobody comes near it, and the pilot is the one who makes that true. If anyone heads toward a turning prop, you stop the airplane and stop the engine. You don't wave, and you don't hope.`],
        terms: [[`PA.XII.A.S2`,`Park in an appropriate area, considering the safety of nearby persons and property.`],[`PA.XII.A.R4`,`Disembarking passengers safely and watching movement on the ramp.`]],
        exam: `Postflight questions are about sequence and documentation: shutdown by checklist, postflight inspection, discrepancies written down.` },
      { h: `The piece of paper`,
        body: [`If you pass, Kendra prints a temporary airman certificate out of IACRA. Under 61.17 a temporary certificate is good for up to 120 days, and it ends early if the permanent card shows up first. The plastic one comes in the mail. Until then the paper is your certificate. 61.3 still applies: certificate, photo ID, and medical, on you or within reach in the airplane, every flight.`,
          `Put it somewhere better than a glovebox. I'm talking to you, the woman who kept a knowledge test report under a tire gauge for three days.`],
        terms: [[`61.17`,`A temporary certificate is issued for up to 120 days, until the permanent one arrives.`]],
        exam: `A temporary airman certificate is valid for 120 days, or until the permanent certificate is received.` },
      { h: `After`,
        body: [`The day you pass, you can carry passengers. To do it legally later, 61.57(a) says three takeoffs and three landings within the preceding 90 days, as the sole manipulator of the controls, in the same category and class. You'll have several on the checkride. Three in 90 days is the floor. You know my opinion of floors. Night passengers need the night version under 61.57(b), full stops between an hour after sunset and an hour before sunrise.`,
          `Your checkride is also your first flight review. 61.56(d) says passing a practical test for a certificate counts. So your 24 calendar months start the month you pass, and run to the end of the 24th month after. Write the month in your logbook in big letters. Then go fly with an instructor long before you have to.`,
          `Before every flight with people in it, 91.107 makes you, the PIC, brief each person on how to fasten and unfasten their seatbelt and shoulder harness, and tell them to wear them for taxi, takeoff, and landing. Each person needs an approved seat with the belt properly secured. A store-bought booster seat is a child restraint the FAA says isn't approved for aircraft. A firm cushion under a belted kid isn't a restraint, and the airplane's belt and harness still have to fit her. The ACS adds the rest of the briefing: who's pilot in command, the doors, how to behave, quiet when you say quiet, the propeller, and what to do in an emergency. Do the whole thing even when it's family. Especially when it's family. Family talks during the takeoff roll.`,
          `91.159 starts mattering now too: above 3,000 feet AGL, magnetic course 0 through 179, odd thousands plus 500; 180 through 359, even thousands plus 500. The Denver shelf over Centennial is still 8,000, and you still don't go into it without hearing the words cleared into the Bravo. You can set your own limits now. The card on the panel used to be my numbers with your name on top. Now it's all yours, and you're the only one who can change it. Change it at a kitchen table, on a day nothing is riding on it, for a reason you can write down. Never in the parking lot.`,
          `And then keep going. An instrument rating, if you want to understand weather from the inside. A tailwheel endorsement, if you want to be humbled by an airplane on purpose. Or just Saturdays in Biscuit with people you love, flown well. That's a whole career too. I'm going to go fly people I'll never meet, and every time somebody says the word checkride I'm going to think about a paramedic who read the remarks section of a TAF out loud to me like it was a patient's chart.`],
        terms: [[`61.57(a)`,`To carry passengers: 3 takeoffs and 3 landings in 90 days, sole manipulator, same category and class.`],[`61.56(d)`,`Passing a practical test counts as a flight review.`],[`91.107(a)`,`PIC briefs each person on belts and harnesses and has them fastened for taxi, takeoff, and landing.`],[`91.159`,`Above 3,000 ft AGL: 0° to 179° magnetic, odd thousands + 500; 180° to 359°, even + 500.`]],
        exam: `Currency questions give a date and a number of landings. Count back 90 days. Flight review questions give a month. Count to the end of the 24th month.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f12s1`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.H`, title:`Dual or nothing`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday, February 8, ten in the morning, the tie down. She'd slept, she'd eaten, and she'd driven to the airport instead of calling anybody, which was what she did now when she wanted to think. Biscuit sat in the cold with her cover on and her tail rope frosted white.`,`Jordan opened the left door, sat sideways in the seat with her boots on the ramp, and looked up at the panel.`,`${(G.nopersonalmins)?`The card above the mag switch was still Abby's. STUDENT SOLO LIMITS (A. STRAND), in Jordan's own handwriting, copied off the endorsement in November. Wind, ceiling, visibility, no passengers, latch both doors. Nothing on it was about March. There'd never been a line on that card that was hers, and this morning, for the first time, she wished there was one, because she knew exactly what it would say.`:`The card above the mag switch had been there since November, white IV tape at the corners, the HI DAD sticker peeking out from under the bottom edge. Her capitals, seven lines. Halfway down, the one she'd written on purpose so she would never have to decide it again: EMOTION IS WEATHER. MARCH AND NOV 5: DUAL OR NOTHING. And at the bottom, the one that held the rest in place: I DON'T CHANGE THIS CARD IN THE PARKING LOT.`}`,`A checkride wasn't dual. Kendra wasn't her instructor. Kendra would be sitting in the right seat with a clipboard, and Jordan would be the pilot in command of an airplane on the twelve month anniversary of the worst phone call of her life, give or take two days. Walt had picked a good slot. Walt didn't know what was in March. Or maybe he did, and he'd decided she'd tell him if it mattered.`,`Her phone was in her hand. Walt's text was still on the screen. Don't make me call her back.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Text Walt yes. A checkride has an examiner aboard, so March doesn't come into it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`She types yes and her thumb stops over the arrow. Doesn't come into it. It's February, and she's already arguing with herself about March. She calls Abby instead. Abby listens to the whole thing and says, <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Maybe you're right. Then be right on purpose, on paper, at a table. Not in your head in a parking lot."</span> They meet that afternoon.`,
          why:`A personal rule interpreted away on the spot is a rule changed under pressure. The rule may need revising, but the revision should be deliberate and written down, before the date, not argued away the moment it gets inconvenient.`},
        {t:`Ask Walt to move it to April, after the anniversary, and take whatever date Kendra can give.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Walt calls her back in ten minutes. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"She's full into May. The year's up in April."</span> A pause she can hear him deciding about. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"I'd give you more time. Russ would. Marisol would. You'd want it in writing, and you'd hate it."</span> He's right. She asks for a day, and that afternoon she sits down with Abby at the club table to decide what March actually means.`,
          why:`Avoiding the anniversary is a legitimate call, and asking is honest. But it treats the date as the problem instead of deciding what a March rule would be for, and the only alternative slot pushes past the deadline. Sitting down to revise the rule on purpose solves both.`},
        {t:`Sit down with Abby before saying yes, and decide what March means.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She texts Walt: <b>Give me until tonight.</b> He answers in a minute. <b>Fine.</b> At two o'clock she and Abby sit at the club table with a fresh index card between them. Abby asks one question, what a March rule would be for, and then doesn't talk for a long time. Jordan answers it: it's for the days she can't trust herself to tell. Not for the month. For the days. They work it out in pencil on the fresh card, and at 4:10 she texts Walt one word. <b>Yes.</b>`,
          why:`Personal minimums can change, and should, when you've learned something. The safe way is the way she did it: before the date, with a second person, by asking what the rule protects, and writing the new version down so the decision isn't made under pressure later.`},
        {t:`Say yes, and plan to decide on the morning of the 19th how she feels.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`It sounds flexible. It's a decision put off to the worst possible morning. Abby, when she hears it, says only, <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"So you'll make the call in the parking lot, at seven fifteen, with Kendra's car pulling in."</span> Jordan hears it. That afternoon they sit at the club table with a fresh index card and do it properly.`,
          why:`Deciding fitness on the morning of a high-pressure event puts the decision exactly where external pressure is strongest. Weather is checked on the day, but the minimums you check it against are set ahead of time.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s2`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.A`, title:`Two point one`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const p=(s.picks||{});const own=(p[0]===2);return [`That evening at her kitchen table she copied it onto a new card in pen, the way they'd worked it out in pencil, and then she drove back out and taped it up herself, on top of the old corners, with fresh IV tape.`,(G.nopersonalmins)?`<prop type="doc" title="Index card, taped above the mag switch">J. REYES. MINE.<br>MARCH AND NOV 5: DUAL, OR AN EXAMINER IN THE RIGHT SEAT.<br>THE WEEKEND OF MARCH 21: NOTHING. NOT EVEN THE AIRPORT.<br>IF I CAN'T SAY OUT LOUD THAT I'M FIT, I CALL THE NIGHT BEFORE.<br>I DON'T CHANGE THIS CARD IN THE PARKING LOT.</prop>`:`<prop type="doc" title="The new index card, taped above the mag switch">J. REYES. PERSONAL MINIMUMS. MINE, NOT THE FAA'S, NOT ABBY'S.<br>SOLO, KAPA PATTERN: CEILING 3,500 AGL. VIS 6 SM. WIND 10, GUSTS 5 OVER, CROSSWIND 5. IF IT'S CLOSE, IT'S NO.<br>7 HOURS OF SLEEP OR I DON'T GO. NO SOLO THE MORNING AFTER A SHIFT.<br>NOTHING TO DRINK FOR 12 HOURS. SICK OR TAKING ANYTHING, I DON'T GO.<br>EMOTION IS WEATHER. MARCH AND NOV 5: DUAL, OR AN EXAMINER IN THE RIGHT SEAT.<br>THE WEEKEND OF MARCH 21: NOTHING. NOT EVEN THE AIRPORT.<br>IF I CAN'T SAY OUT LOUD THAT I'M FIT, I CALL THE NIGHT BEFORE.<br>ONE FACTOR: ADD 500 FT, 1/2 MILE, TAKE 5 KNOTS OFF. TWO FACTORS: GO HOME.<br>I DON'T CHANGE THIS CARD IN THE PARKING LOT.</prop>`,(G.nopersonalmins)?`It was the first card with nothing of Abby's on it. Abby's limits went into the back of the logbook, folded, where they still applied, because she was still a student. ${own?`Abby had read it twice at the table and said it was short. Jordan said short was the point.`:`Abby read it twice and said it was short. Jordan said short was the point.`}`:`${own?`Abby had made her read it out loud twice at the table, and then said the old line had been a good rule for October, and this was a better one for March.`:`Abby made her read it out loud twice and said the old line had been a good rule for October, and this was a better one for March.`}`,`Then the back page of the logbook, because two numbers were still open.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Jordan's logbook, back page: 61.109(a), as of 2/8/27">TOTAL ${(G.pushedweather)?`61.8`:`61.1`} (40) ✓. DUAL ${(G.pushedweather)?`53.9`:`53.2`} (20) ✓. <b>SOLO 7.9 (10). 2.1 TO GO.</b><br>XC DUAL 4.4 ✓. NIGHT ${(G.pushedweather)?`6.3`:`5.6`} ✓. NIGHT FULL STOPS ${(G.pushedweather)?`13`:`12`} ✓. HOOD 3.1 ✓. SOLO XC 5.1 ✓. LONG SOLO XC ✓. TOWERED ✓.<br><b>CHECKRIDE PREP 3.0 WITHIN 2 CAL MONTHS: 0.0. JAN, FEB, OR MAR COUNT FOR A MARCH TEST.</b><br>SOLO ENDORSEMENT (A.7) 1/6/27, 90 DAYS.<br>KNOWLEDGE TEST 1/12/27: 95 ✓. A.2 1/21 ✓. A.1, A.37: AT THE END.</prop>`,`Abby had blocked out the prep: February 17, March 4, March 10, March 16, all dual, all on days that came after a full night of sleep, which she'd worked out from a photo of the station fridge. The solo time was Jordan's to plan.`,`Down in the flight bag, folded soft along the creases, was Danny's 2018 sectional, the one with the coffee ring on Castle Rock and the pencil lines going out from Centennial like spokes. Moab. Steamboat. And one short straight line northeast, to a magenta circle with tick marks around it, at a town called Akron. Eighty four miles, give or take. A little less than an hour each way. She'd measured it once already, in January, and hadn't told anybody.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Fly all 2.1 at once in the practice area the morning of the 10th, right off her shift, her one open morning.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`She has it half planned when she remembers the one thing Abby has said a hundred times: never solo the morning after a twenty four. The 10th is the morning after a shift. She closes the calendar, picks two days that come after a real night of sleep, and feels slightly stupid in a way she's learned to be grateful for.`,
          why:`Fatigue is the IMSAFE item that feels most like being fine. The morning after a 24 hour shift is exactly what her personal minimums rule out for solo flight, and spreading the time across rested days costs nothing.`},
        {t:`Ask Abby to endorse a solo cross-country to Akron, and fly the line Danny drew.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"I'd sign it,"</span> Abby says. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Planning reviewed, endorsement for that flight, same as Limon. It's legal and you'd fly it fine."</span> Then, carefully: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Is that the trip you want to fly alone?"</span> Jordan thinks about the pencil line, and about who it was drawn for, and books the practice area instead. Akron can wait for the people it's for.`,
          why:`With the 61.93 endorsement for that specific flight, a solo cross-country to Akron would be legal and safe. But the remaining solo time is better spent on the maneuvers she'll be tested on, and this particular route has a purpose she'd be using up early.`},
        {t:`Two solos in the practice area on rested days, built around short fields and slow flight.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Thursday, February 11: 1.2, slow flight over the reservoir until the stall horn chirped and quit and chirped and quit on command, then six short field landings on 35R with Abby's point, the first stripe past the numbers, written on her kneeboard. Tuesday, February 23: 1.1, the same, plus steep turns until the bump of her own wake came through the floor twice. She writes both in the logbook in ink and doesn't update the back page until she's added it twice.`,
          why:`The remaining solo time has to be logged anyway, so using it to rehearse the tasks she'll be graded on, on days she's rested, turns a requirement into preparation. That's what the time is for.`},
        {t:`Fly Danny's line to Akron solo on the 11th. She flew 207 miles alone in December, so 84 is easy.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`She's drawing the line on a new sectional when she realizes what's missing. The Limon trip had a green endorsement in her logbook for that flight, written after Abby went through her planning. This one wouldn't. 61.93(c) wants one for each solo cross-country, every time, and her distance record doesn't carry over. She puts the plotter down and books the practice area.`,
          why:`A student pilot needs an instructor's endorsement for each solo cross-country flight after the instructor reviews the planning (61.93(c)(3)). Earlier experience doesn't substitute for it, and a flight without it would be a violation.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s3`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.A`, title:`The offer`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday, March 15, 6:50 in the evening. Daylight saving time had started the day before, which meant Jordan had lost an hour of sleep the night before a 24 and never gotten it back, and the sun was still up over the mountains when she came out of the grocery store. Her phone rang in the parking lot. Abby never called. Abby texted, in three parts.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"I got it."</span> She didn't wait for Jordan to ask what. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"The regional. The number with the weird area code in January was the recruiter. I interviewed in February and didn't tell you, because you had a written and then you had a checkride and I'm a professional. Offer came this afternoon. Class starts Monday the 22nd, out of state. I fly out Sunday."</span>`,`She'd crossed fifteen hundred hours on a Tuesday in March and told nobody. Jordan stood in the parking lot with a bag of groceries on her hip. A cart rolled past her on its own, slowly, toward a Honda.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Your checkride's Friday. I'll be at the fence. I'm flying your last prep tomorrow. After the flight I sign A.1 and A.37, and your recommendation in IACRA. So that's fine."</span> A pause. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"I'm telling you before I tell my mom. That's not a pep talk. It's a fact."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask her to push her start date, since this checkride is the payoff for both of them.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`It's out before she can catch it. There's a silence on the line, and then Abby says, evenly, <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"You don't get to ask me that."</span> Jordan knows it the second she hears it. She says sorry, and congratulations, and means both, and they talk about the handoff instead. Whatever Abby does with her start date, it'll be Abby's.`,
          why:`An airline class date is a career event, not a scheduling detail. Asking her instructor to risk it for a test the paperwork already covers puts Jordan's wants on someone else, which isn't her decision to make.`},
        {t:`Congratulate her, and leave the paperwork to Abby. She's done this for dozens of students.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Abby. That's huge. Go."</span> They talk about the airline for ten minutes, the base Abby wants, the uniform she already hates. It's only later, putting away the eggs, that Jordan realizes she has no idea who signs anything if Friday slips. She texts. Abby answers fast: <b>Good question. Should've been yours an hour ago. Tomorrow.</b>`,
          why:`Celebrating is right. But a checkride can slip for weather, and the applicant needs to know who will give any further training or endorsements if her instructor is gone. Asking for the handoff protects the plan.`},
        {t:`Congratulate her, and ask for the handoff she promised in April, in case Friday slips.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Abby. That's huge. Congratulations."</span> Then: <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"You said in April I'd get a handoff, your notes, and a CFI you trust. If it snows Friday, who's that?"</span> A short laugh on the line. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"You remember that. Of course you do."</span> Tuesday after the prep flight, Abby hands her a printed page with a name and number on it, Gabe at the north end, and her training notes in a clear sleeve, and signs both endorsements in green while Jordan watches the pen.`,
          why:`She took the news for what it was, and then made sure her own plan would survive a weather delay: who would give training and endorsements if the test slipped. That's managing a known risk without asking someone else to carry it.`},
        {t:`Tell her to go now, and fly the last prep with another instructor so Abby isn't stuck.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"I'm not stuck. I'm flying your prep tomorrow at nine."</span> She sounds almost offended. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"You don't hand off a student four days before a checkride to be polite. That's how people walk in with an instructor who's never seen them land."</span> Jordan apologizes. Abby flies the prep.`,
          why:`Changing instructors days before the test, when the current instructor is available, adds risk for no gain. The new instructor wouldn't know her flying or her missed areas, and the 61.39 endorsement depends on that knowledge.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s4`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.A`, title:`The column`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday, March 19, 7:24 AM. The FBO's second briefing room had a round table, four chairs that didn't match, a window onto the ramp, and a whiteboard somebody had cleaned badly, so that the ghost of a holding pattern was still on it. The sky outside was a low flat gray, and the windsock was out straight from the northeast.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Weather, printed at 7:05 AM"><code>METAR KAPA 191253Z 03009KT 10SM OVC025 M01/M04 A3021 RMK AO2<br><br>TAF KAPA 191120Z 1912/2012 03010KT P6SM OVC025<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;FM191600 03014G22KT 2SM -SN BR OVC012<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;FM192000 02015G25KT 3/4SM SN OVC006<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;FM200500 36010KT 3SM -SN OVC015</code></prop>`,`Upslope. She'd seen it Tuesday in the forecast discussion and Thursday on every channel. Daylight time now, UTC minus six, so the snow was due at ten.`,`Kendra Lowe came in at 7:29 with a rolling bag, a laptop, a thermos with a fishing lure sticker on it, and a gray braid down her back. She was somewhere in her fifties, compact, with reading glasses pushed up into her hair, and she shook hands like someone who'd decided long ago not to make a production of it. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Jordan. Kendra. Sit wherever you like. We'll start with paper, because paper is where people lose the most time."</span>`,`She paid Kendra's fee first, by check, the number Walt had told her in February with the face he used for weather. Then the paper. Kendra pulled up the application in IACRA with the FTN and turned the laptop so Jordan could see it. Photo ID. The student pilot certificate, plastic, a little bent. The medical. The knowledge test report, out of its sleeve, and Kendra ran her thumb over the raised seal the way the proctor had. The logbook, open to the endorsements, A.2 from January and A.1 and A.37 from Tuesday in Abby's green.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Jordan's logbook, endorsements">03/16/27. I certify that Jordan E. Reyes has received and logged training time within 2 calendar months preceding the month of application in preparation for the practical test and they are prepared for the required practical test for the issuance of a private pilot certificate.<br>/s/ 03/16/27 A. Strand 3418862CFI RE 06-30-2027<br><br>03/16/27. I certify that Jordan E. Reyes has received the required training in accordance with 14 CFR §§ 61.107 and 61.109. I have determined they are prepared for the Private Pilot Airplane Single-Engine Land practical test.<br>/s/ 03/16/27 A. Strand 3418862CFI RE 06-30-2027</prop>`,`Then 61.109, line by line. Kendra read each number off the application and found it in the logbook herself. Total. Dual. Night. Hood. The long solo cross-country. Prep: four flights with Abby, February 17 and three in March, 4.6 hours. Then solo.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Application says solo 10.2. Your back page says 10.1."</span> She said it the way you'd mention someone's shoelace was untied.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell her the back page is a running tally and the application is right.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`Kendra nods. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Maybe. Show me."</span> And waits. So Jordan does what she should have done first: adds the entries, one by one, out loud. The application is right. The back page is wrong, because on February 23 she wrote 1.0 on the summary and 1.1 in the entry. Kendra watches the whole thing without a word and then makes a small note.`,
          why:`Asserting that one record is right without checking it is a guess. When two records disagree, the fix is to go back to the source entries and add them, which is what 61.39(a)(3) has the examiner verifying.`},
        {t:`Add the solo column again from the entries, in front of her, and fix whichever one is wrong.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Let me add it."</span> She runs a finger down the solo column out loud, the way she reads back a drug and a dose. The October solo. November. December. Limon. February 11, 1.2. February 23, entry 1.1, summary 1.0. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"The summary's wrong. 10.2. The application's right."</span> She corrects the back page in ink, one line through the old number, initials it. Kendra watches, then writes something down. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"The back page is yours. The entries are the record. Good."</span>`,
          why:`The logbook entries are the record of aeronautical experience. Recomputing from the entries, in front of the examiner, settles the discrepancy, and correcting the summary with a single line and initials keeps the record honest.`},
        {t:`Ask if they can call Abby, since she signed the recommendation and knows the numbers.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"We can. She's not the one taking the test."</span> Kendra slides the logbook back across. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"These are your entries. What do they say?"</span> Jordan adds the column. The back page is off by a tenth, February 23. The application is right. She fixes the summary in ink.`,
          why:`The instructor reviewed the application, but the applicant owns the logbook and must be able to show the experience. Calling for help isn't wrong, it's just slower than adding the entries herself.`},
        {t:`Change the back page to 10.2 so the two records match, and move on.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`She's reaching for the pen when Kendra puts two fingers on the page. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"How do you know 10.2 is the right one?"</span> Jordan doesn't, yet. She adds the entries. It is the right one, this time. Kendra lets her fix it, and says, <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Make them agree by checking, not by copying."</span>`,
          why:`Changing one record to match another without verifying either just moves the error. The entries have to be added before anything is corrected.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s5`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.F`, title:`Seventy nine pounds`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`8:20 AM. Kendra had read the airplane's records for half an hour, the annual, the ELT, the transponder, and the AD list with the selector line now in Marisol's ink, done January 5, next due at 100 hours on the tach. She asked about the left fuel cap from December. She asked what Biscuit's door did. Then she closed the binder.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Walt tells me you've been planning a trip for a while. Where is it?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Akron. Colorado Plains Regional. My niece, and her mom in back."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Then that's our scenario. You passed today. It's a couple of weeks from now, a Saturday morning. You, your niece in the right seat, her mother in the back. Full tanks. Show me the airplane can do it."</span> She pushed a blank weight and balance form across the table. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Start with people."</span>`,`${(G.skippedwb)?`Jordan's pencil stopped on the line for the right seat. She remembered a Saturday in August and two empty lines on a clipboard, and the word sure, and Abby's hand held out for it. She'd been sure. The total hadn't been what she was sure of. Her ears went hot, the way they had then.`:`Jordan had done this worksheet more times than she could count since August, on the hood of Claire's car, on the club table, once on a napkin. This time a stranger was watching the pencil.`}`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Use Ellie's 76 from August, add a few pounds, and say she'll weigh everyone before the real flight.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"That's honest,"</span> Kendra says. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"How many is a few?"</span> Jordan says five. Kendra writes 81 on the line in her own hand. Jordan finishes. Well inside the envelope, CG and weight both. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"For a planning question, fine. For the Saturday, I'd want the real number. Kids grow in spurts."</span>`,
          why:`A conservative estimate for planning, with a commitment to weigh before the flight, is a reasonable answer. Actual weights are better when they're available, and a growing child's weight from seven months ago isn't current data.`},
        {t:`Use the numbers from Marisol's scale on Saturday: Ellie 79 in sneakers, seat cushion 3, Claire 174.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"I weighed them Saturday. Marisol's scale. Ellie was 79 with her shoes on, the seat cushion's 3, Claire's 174. I'm 162. Forty gallons."</span> She does it in pencil and Kendra checks every line with a calculator. 2,156 pounds, CG 43.18, forward limit 37.06 at that weight, 144 under gross. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Why weigh a seat cushion?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Because it's in the airplane."</span> Kendra comes very close to smiling. Not quite. Next: where does the CG go as the fuel burns off? Forward, a little; fuel sits aft of it. Then Akron's runway, and the PAPIs.`,
          why:`Actual weights, including small items, produce a real answer instead of an estimate. She also knew her numbers before she needed them, which is what PA.I.F asks: compute weight and balance and determine that the airplane is within limits for the planned flight.`},
        {t:`Use 170 for Claire and 80 for Ellie. Those are standard weights, and close for planning.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Standard according to whom?"</span> Jordan doesn't have an answer that applies to a 172 on a Saturday. Kendra waits. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Do you know what they weigh?"</span> She does, actually. She weighed them Saturday. She erases and writes the real numbers in.`,
          why:`Average passenger weights are used in some commercial programs under specific approvals. For a private flight in a four seat airplane, the pilot should use actual weights, especially near the limits.`},
        {t:`Skip the line. Two adults and a kid are nowhere near max gross in a 172.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Kendra doesn't say anything. She turns the blank form a quarter inch so it's square to Jordan. Waits. Jordan fills it in. It's fine this time, 144 under. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Nowhere near isn't a number."</span> A note on the clipboard. Jordan could swear she's heard that sentence before. Different voice. Same table, nearly.`,
          why:`Weight and balance has to be computed, not assumed. Two adults, a child, and full fuel in a 172 can get close to the limit, and CG position matters even when weight is fine.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s6`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`III.A`, title:`The step`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`By nine the oral had a rhythm. Kendra asked. Kendra waited. The heater ticked.`,`She did the intercepts first, as promised. What do you do if a fighter shows up off your left wing, a little ahead, and rocks its wings? Jordan said: rock hers back to acknowledge, and follow it. Squawk 7700 unless ATC says otherwise, and try to call on guard, the emergency frequency, with who she is and where. A climbing turn of ninety degrees or more away from her, without crossing her path, means she can go. Circling the airport and overflying the runway means land there. Kendra made her do the hand motions. Jordan felt ridiculous and got every one.`,`Then the chart. Kendra put her finger on the magenta circle northeast of Denver, the one with tick marks around it and a small star. Jordan said: magenta means no tower. The ticks mean fuel and the field's attended during normal working hours. The star means a rotating beacon, sunset to sunrise. She checked it against the chart supplement entry for Akron on her tablet while Kendra watched, and said attended eight to five, beacon, 100LL around the clock by card. Kendra nodded once.`,`Then she turned the sectional over, so it was just paper.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Akron. You've landed and shut down. Ellie's getting out on her side. She's on the step, she slips on frost, and she lands on her hand. Her wrist is broken. Claire's an ER nurse, so you know it's broken. What is that, and what do you do?"</span>`,`It was the question. Jordan knew it the way you know a rhythm on a monitor. A passenger with a broken bone, the one she'd answered as a paramedic in January.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`An accident: she was still getting out, and a broken wrist is serious. Notify the NTSB now, report in 10 days.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`She says it slowly. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"It's an accident under 830.2. It happened between boarding and when everyone has gotten off, and she hadn't gotten off. A broken wrist is a serious injury, because the only fractures that aren't are simple ones of fingers, toes, and the nose. So after Ellie's taken care of, I notify the NTSB immediately, under 830.5, and file the report within 10 days under 830.15. And I don't move the airplane."</span> Kendra writes for a while. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Your report said you missed this one."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"I answered it as a medic."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"And now?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Now I answered it as the pilot."</span>`,
          why:`Under 49 CFR 830.2, an accident includes serious injury between boarding with intent of flight and everyone disembarking. A wrist fracture is a serious injury. 830.5 requires immediate notification, and 830.15 a written report within 10 days.`},
        {t:`An accident, but take care of Ellie first and then notify the NTSB within 30 days, by written report.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Ellie first, yes. Always. Then when?"</span> Jordan hears the 30 and knows it's wrong as she says it. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Immediately. The notification's immediate. The written report is 10 days."</span> Kendra lets her correct it, and asks two more NTSB questions to see if the first answer was a slip. It was.`,
          why:`She classified it correctly and put the patient first, which is right. But 830.5 requires immediate notification of an accident, and 830.15 sets the written report at 10 days, not 30.`},
        {t:`Not an accident. The engine was off and the airplane was parked, so the flight was over.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"When does an accident window close?"</span> Jordan opens her mouth and finds the definition in her head. When all such persons have disembarked. Ellie was on the step. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"It's an accident."</span> Kendra asks her to say the rest, and she does, carefully, and Kendra writes for a long time.`,
          why:`The 830.2 window runs from boarding with the intention of flight until all persons have disembarked, not until the engine stops. A passenger injured while getting out is still inside it.`},
        {t:`It's only an accident if she's in the hospital for more than 48 hours.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"That's one way to be seriously injured. Is it the only one?"</span> It isn't, and Jordan knows the list. Fractures. Except simple fingers, toes, nose. She says it, and the rest, and Kendra writes for a while and then asks her a different one about a passenger with a cut that needs stitches.`,
          why:`Hospitalization over 48 hours is one of several criteria. Any fracture except simple fractures of fingers, toes, or nose is a serious injury on its own under 830.2.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s7`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.C`, title:`Upslope`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`10:25 AM. The oral had run two hours and fifty five minutes, and Kendra had just said the words Jordan had been waiting for since February: <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"That's the ground portion. You passed it."</span> She'd said it the way she said everything, without decoration, and then she'd looked out the window.`,`Jordan looked too. The ramp had gone soft and gray. The mountains had been gone since nine. Snow was coming sideways past the light poles, small and dry and determined, and the windsock was standing out stiff from the northeast.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Weather, printed at 10:12 AM"><code>METAR KAPA 191553Z 03014G21KT 2SM -SN BR OVC011 M02/M04 A3018 RMK AO2<br><br>METAR KCFO 191553Z 03016G24KT 1 1/2SM -SN BR OVC009 M03/M04 A3017<br><br>TAF KAPA 191520Z AMD 1915/2012 03014G22KT 2SM -SN BR OVC011<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;FM191900 02016G26KT 1/2SM SN FG VV004<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;FM200500 36010KT 3SM -SN OVC015</code></prop>`,`Eleven hundred feet. Two miles. Gusts to 21 and building. The amendment had brought the worst of it in an hour early, which was what amendments did on days like this.`,`${(G.pushedweather)?`She knew what this felt like from the inside, and that was the problem. A ceiling that was legal. A deck that was coming down. A window, if you wanted to call it that, and a person waiting who'd driven in for you. The last time she'd looked at a sky like this and seen a window, she'd watched the I-25 headlights grow halos over Larkspur, and then go out, and heard Abby say my airplane in a voice with nothing in it. She'd written that night down in her own logbook in two words, the way Abby had told her to.`:`She'd looked at a sky like this one in December, at the tie down, with her mother's birthday on the other end of it. She'd turned around then with the tanks full. It had been the cheapest place to do it, and she'd never once wished she'd gone.`}`,`Kendra turned from the window. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"You're pilot in command of that airplane today. Are we flying?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask to fly now, before it gets worse, and get the pattern work done in the hour they've got.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"No,"</span> Kendra says, before Jordan has finished the sentence. Not unkindly. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"We'd be in the pattern under eleven hundred feet in snow with a TAF that says it's going down, so you can tell your examiner you got the landings in. I'd be discontinuing it from the runway."</span> She opens her laptop. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"That was the last question of the oral. I've noted the answer."</span>`,
          why:`Racing deteriorating weather to finish a task is the classic setup for a weather accident. The ceiling and visibility were already low for maneuvering, and the amended TAF showed the trend going the wrong way. Discontinuing preserves credit for what's done.`},
        {t:`Wait in the lobby until noon and see if it lifts. The airplane's ready and so is she.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"We could. What does the TAF say about noon?"</span> Jordan reads it again. One o'clock, half a mile and snow. There's no noon in it that isn't worse. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"It doesn't lift."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"No. So let's not sit in a lobby hoping it says something else."</span> She writes the letter.`,
          why:`Waiting for improvement isn't unsafe, and it's often right. Here, though, the forecast and the trend both said the weather would get worse through the afternoon, so waiting just postpones the decision the data already makes.`},
        {t:`Tell Kendra they aren't flying today, and ask for a letter of discontinuance for the oral.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:6},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"We're not flying. Eleven hundred and two, gusts building, and the amendment brings it down by one. I don't want to fly maneuvers under that, and I don't want to be the pilot who tried. Can I get a letter of discontinuance for the oral?"</span> Kendra's already opening her laptop. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"You can. Under 61.43(e), for weather. Area I's done. You'll keep it for 60 days."</span> She looks at Jordan over the screen. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"For the record, I was going to say no too. I like it better when I don't have to."</span>`,
          why:`The weather was below safe conditions for the flight portion and forecast to worsen. As PIC she made the call and knew the right tool: 61.43(e)(2) allows discontinuing for weather, and the letter of discontinuance preserves credit for the completed areas for 60 days.`},
        {t:`Ask Kendra to make the call. She's the examiner and she's seen a thousand of these days.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Kendra shakes her head. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"I'm not the pilot in command. You are. 61.47 says so, and so do I."</span> She waits. Jordan looks out the window again, at the snow going sideways past the light poles, and says it: they're not flying. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Good. That's the answer I'd have wanted from you with Ellie in the seat."</span> She writes the letter.`,
          why:`The examiner isn't pilot in command unless agreed in advance (61.47(b)). Handing the go/no-go decision to her abdicates the PIC's job, and the ACS treats a failure to use ADM as a failed task.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s8`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`II`, title:`Clean wings`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`<prop type="doc" title="Letter of Discontinuance, 03/19/2027">APPLICANT: JORDAN E. REYES<br>PRACTICAL TEST: PRIVATE PILOT, AIRPLANE SINGLE-ENGINE LAND<br>DISCONTINUED 03/19/2027, WEATHER (14 CFR 61.43(e)(2))<br>AREAS OF OPERATION SATISFACTORILY COMPLETED: I. PREFLIGHT PREPARATION<br>CREDIT FOR COMPLETED AREAS IF THE REMAINDER IS PASSED BY 05/18/2027 (61.43(f))<br>PRESENT THIS LETTER WHEN THE TEST RESUMES.<br>/s/ K. LOWE, DPE</prop>`,`Kendra had a cancellation, Monday, March 22, at eight, and she offered it. Jordan had looked at Monday for a long time Friday afternoon, at the weekend sitting in front of it, and then at her card. The weekend was nothing, not even the airport, and Monday wasn't the weekend. She'd said yes.`,`Friday night, at 9:52, a text from Abby.`,`<prop type="text" from="Abby Strand" time="9:52 PM">Called the airline. There's a class on the 29th. I'm in it. That's a week I'm buying for me, not for you, so don't thank me. I'll be on the ramp Monday at 8. Don't make it weird.</prop>`,`Sunday was a year. She came off a twenty four at seven. They went to the nine o'clock Mass at her mother's church in Aurora, all of them, and then to Claire's, where Ellie had a game at two in the slush and played the whole thing at the back like a wall, and nobody watched the sideline, and everybody watched the sideline. Jordan didn't go to the airport. She'd written that down.`,`Monday, March 22, 7:05 AM. Clear, still, minus four, the sky scrubbed blue the way it only got after an upslope storm, and eight inches of new snow on everything that wasn't the ramp. Marisol had swept Biscuit's wings Sunday afternoon and put the cover back on. Overnight the cold had found the gap at the tail.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Weather, printed at 6:58 AM"><code>METAR KAPA 221253Z 34006KT 10SM CLR M04/M09 A3034 RMK AO2<br><br>TAF KAPA 221120Z 2212/2312 VRB04KT P6SM SKC<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;FM221800 34010KT P6SM FEW120</code></prop>`,`${(G.rushedpreflight)?`Kendra walked out with her at 7:20 and stood at the tail with her thermos while Jordan worked down the checklist, and she didn't say anything at all, which was louder. At the cowling Jordan pulled the dipstick, read it, wiped it, put it back, pulled it again, and read it again. Kendra noticed. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Twice?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"I skipped it once. Last May. Marisol had just changed the oil and I trusted the logbook instead of the stick. Nothing was wrong. I was wrong."</span> Kendra nodded slowly. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Most people learn that one later, and it costs more."</span> She wrote something down. Jordan decided not to wonder what.`:`Kendra walked out with her at 7:20 and stood at the tail with her thermos while Jordan worked down the checklist, reading each line and touching each thing. She didn't say anything. Every so often she looked at something Jordan had just looked at.`}`,`Over by the FBO door, behind the painted line, three people were standing in the cold with coffee. Walt was at the fence by the gate, collar up, in the spot he'd stood in since September. Abby was by the door with her hands jammed in her jacket. And next to her, in the good maroon cardigan under a long wool coat, with a scarf wound twice around her neck, stood Jordan's mother, who had never once come to watch Danny take off. Not in eleven years. Walt had gone and picked her up in Aurora at six thirty. Jordan had found that out from Claire, by text, at 6:41.`,`At the tail she ran a glove along the top of the horizontal stabilizer. White. Sometime overnight the cover had flapped loose at the back. So: frost, stabilizer and elevator both, thin, gritty, like a nail file. Right wing too, a gray strip along the trailing edge. You could miss it from six feet. Up close it looked like 220 grit.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Leave it. It's paper thin, and it'll be gone by the end of a ten thousand foot takeoff roll.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Will it?"</span> Kendra says. Just that. Jordan looks at the stabilizer again, at minus four with no sun on it yet, and hears herself. At minus four, in March? It isn't going anywhere. It'll be on the tail at rotation, on the one surface whose job is the nose. She goes for the brush.`,
          why:`The PHAK says frost disrupts airflow, reduces lift, and increases drag, and that an aircraft must be thoroughly cleaned and free of frost before flight. Thin frost is enough to change stall behavior, and it won't reliably come off during a takeoff in freezing air.`},
        {t:`Tell Kendra she needs twenty minutes, and get every bit off the wings and the tail by hand.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:6},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Frost on the stabilizer and the right wing. I need twenty minutes. Maybe thirty."</span> Kendra nods, takes her thermos, and finds some sun to stand in. Before Jordan can even finish asking, there's Marisol, brush in one hand, towels in the other. (Hangar door. She'd been watching since 7:15.) Brush, towel, then Jordan's bare hand over every square foot of both wings and the tail, Abby's rule from October, until her fingers hurt and then a bit past that. Clean. The checkride starts thirty one minutes late. Over by the FBO her mother checks her watch, opens her mouth, shuts it.`,
          why:`An aircraft must be free of frost before flight. She found it on the walkaround, said so, took the time, and confirmed by touch. Delaying the test to fix a hazard is exactly the preflight assessment the ACS asks for (PA.II.A.S2, verify the airplane is in condition for safe flight).`},
        {t:`Ask Marisol to tow Biscuit into the warm hangar, let the frost melt, then fly late.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:2},
          after:`Twenty minutes in the warm hangar and the frost is water. Marisol tosses her two towels. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Dry every hinge. Take her back out into minus four wet and the water finds the elevator hinges and freezes right where you don't want it."</span> Hinge by hinge, then. Back out an hour late. Kendra spends the hour on carbon monoxide questions, which seems fair.`,
          why:`Melting frost in a heated hangar is a real method, and it works. But water left on the surfaces or in the control hinges can refreeze in subfreezing air, so the airplane has to be dried completely before it goes back out. Removing it by hand, outside, avoided that problem.`},
        {t:`Clean the right wing and leave the tail. The stabilizer pushes down, it doesn't hold the airplane up.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Halfway down the wing she hears it. Doesn't lift? The stabilizer's an airfoil. Upside down, sort of, but an airfoil, and it's the thing holding the nose where she wants it at rotation. Back to the tail with the brush. Kendra hasn't said a word. Kendra writes something small.`,
          why:`The horizontal stabilizer is a lifting surface that provides the tail's downforce and pitch control. Frost there can degrade pitch authority. Every surface has to be clean before flight, not only the wing.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s9`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`, task:`VI`, title:`Akron goes low`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`8:31 AM. Engine start. Mixture, throttle, prime, master, the beacon, clear prop, and Biscuit coughed twice in the cold and caught. Taxi to Alpha. Runup near the departure end of 35R, mags, carb heat, the door latches, the top one on her side twice. Kendra watched her do the top one twice and didn't comment. Jordan told ground she was ready for departure, and ground told her to monitor tower.`,`She briefed the exchange of controls out loud before they took the runway, the way the ACS wanted it, three steps, you have the flight controls, I have the flight controls, you have the flight controls. Kendra said it back to her. Then tower cleared Niner One Echo for takeoff on 35R.`,`Full power. Ten thousand feet of runway in front of her, and in air this cold and dense Biscuit needed about a tenth of it. She rotated at fifty five. The door stayed shut. Over the left wing as she climbed, the FBO slid by and she could see three small figures on the ramp, and one of them had a hand up, and it wasn't Walt's, because Walt's was at the fence, and it wasn't Abby's, because Abby never waved at anything.`,`Right turn east. Climbing to seven thousand five hundred. She said it out loud, the way she'd been saying it since July. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Shelf at eighty. I'm staying at seventy five."</span> Flight following with Denver Approach, radar contact, which wasn't a yes to anything. The Aurora Reservoir under the left wing, a sheet of white ice with a black crack across it. BEND, her turn point from November, on the GPS and out the window at the same time. Then northeast, on Danny's line.`,`First checkpoint: on time, almost to the minute, which felt like cheating. At the second one she checked groundspeed. Eleven point two miles, six minutes fifteen. She did it in her head. Kendra did it on a calculator. A hundred and seven. Same number, so nobody had to say anything.`,`East of Strasburg. I-70 under the left wing, a truck stop, snow on the fields in long stripes where the plow had thrown it. Kendra closed the binder.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"New information. Akron's six hundred overcast now. Going down, not up. Where to?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep heading for Akron for now. It's an hour of flying, and things can lift by the time they get there.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Can they."</span> Not quite a question. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"What did you just tell me Friday about hoping a forecast says something else?"</span> Jordan's hand is already moving the heading bug. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"I'm diverting. Colorado Air and Space Port."</span> She turns, and it costs her a minute she'll notice on the estimate.`,
          why:`Continuing toward a destination that has gone below VFR on the hope it improves is the decision that diversion planning exists to replace. The ACS wants a prompt, reasoned choice of an alternate and a route to it.`},
        {t:`Turn toward Colorado Air and Space Port now, then work out the heading, time, and fuel, and tell Denver.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Diverting to the Space Port."</span> Left turn, already rolling into it while she's talking. The Space Port is out there to the west, a gray cross on white, big enough to see from here. Level. Then the arithmetic. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"About fourteen miles, two eighty true, call it two seventy five magnetic. Into this wind maybe a hundred knots. Eight or nine minutes, about a gallon. Under the seven thousand floor before I get close, so I'm starting down to sixty five hundred now."</span> Then Denver, then the ATIS. Kendra writes for the length of the descent.`,
          why:`PA.VI.C: turn toward a suitable alternate promptly, then estimate heading, groundspeed, time, and fuel, and use ATC and automation. Turning first and refining after keeps the airplane moving away from the problem while the planning catches up.`},
        {t:`Pick the nearest airport on the GPS and go direct to whatever comes up first.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Top of the nearest page: a private grass strip three miles off. Under eight inches of snow, presumably, like everything else today. She scrolls. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Not that one. The Space Port. Fourteen miles, towered, long runways."</span> She turns. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Nearest isn't always suitable,"</span> Kendra says, and writes.`,
          why:`The GPS nearest function is a real resource, and using it is reasonable. But the alternate has to be suitable for the conditions and the airplane, which means checking what comes up rather than accepting the first line.`},
        {t:`Hold heading while she measures the new course exactly with the plotter on the sectional.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`She has the plotter on her knee and her head down for forty seconds, and when she looks up the heading's wandered fifteen degrees and Kendra is watching the altimeter, not her. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Where are we?"</span> Jordan turns west first, then finishes the estimate with her eyes up. The ACS gives her fifteen degrees. She'd used all of it.`,
          why:`Precision can come after the turn. Heads-down plotting while holding the old course delays the diversion and lets altitude and heading drift, which are graded at ±200 feet and ±15 degrees during a diversion.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s10`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`IV`, title:`Two hundred feet`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The Space Port tower gave her a right base for 35. Eight thousand feet of runway with snow banked along both edges like the sides of a bobsled run. Kendra asked for a soft field landing, so Jordan flew it like the runway was mud: a little power in the flare, nosewheel held off as long as the elevator would hold it, then the soft field takeoff, nose high, off in ground effect, level, accelerate, climb. Kendra didn't say anything. Kendra wrote.`,`Hood on. East, out past the ten mile ring, under the eight thousand floor, into a sun she couldn't see. Straight and level. A climb. A descent. Turns to headings. Then Kendra said, <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Close your eyes and put your head down,"</span> and flew the airplane somewhere unpleasant, and said, <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Your airplane."</span> ${(G.pushedweather)?`Jordan opened her eyes on a nose low left bank with the airspeed climbing and for half a second she was over Larkspur again, in the snow, with the highway gone and Abby's hand on the yoke. Then she was here. Power back. Level the wings on the attitude indicator. Ease the nose up to the horizon. The same three things Abby had done that night, in that order, in a voice with nothing in it. She'd watched every one.`:`Jordan opened her eyes on a nose low left bank with the airspeed climbing. Power back. Level the wings on the attitude indicator. Ease the nose up to the horizon. In that order, which Abby had made her say out loud forty times in January until it bored her, which was the point.`}`,`Hood off. An emergency descent. Then, over a white quarter section with a farm road along one side, Kendra pulled the throttle to idle and said the engine had quit, and Jordan had the nose at sixty five knots before Kendra's hand was back in her lap, picked the field upwind of the road, flew the checklist, and had them set up for a landing in the snow by the time Kendra said, <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Good. Go around."</span>`,`Slow flight at seventy five hundred, with the stall horn chirping, held within forty feet. A power off stall. A power on stall, the left wing trying to drop and her right foot telling it not to. Steep turns: left, lost forty feet; right, gained sixty, felt her own wake bump through the floor, rolled out four degrees past east and fixed it. Turns around a center pivot buried in snow, the wind drifting her toward the road until she steepened the bank downwind. Kendra didn't say anything about any of it. By then Jordan had decided that was a compliment.`,`Back to Centennial. A forward slip to a landing on 35R, the airplane crabbed sideways and dropping like an elevator, straightened at the flare, down on the second set of stripes. A go-around Kendra called at two hundred feet, power, carb heat in, flaps up in steps, climb. Then tower cleared them for the option, and Kendra said the last thing on her list.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Short field. Your point is the numbers. On them, or no more than two hundred feet past."</span>`,`Final. Sixty knots, flaps forty, the 35 parked dead still in the windshield. Still is good. Still means you hit it. Then at two hundred feet a bump off the snowbanks picked Biscuit up from underneath. Sixty nine knots. Twenty feet high. And the numbers started sliding, down the glass, under the cowling, gone. ${(G.forcedlanding)?`She knew this exact feeling from the end of September, on 28, with the wind, and wanting it so much she'd tried to make the runway take it.`:`Float. She knew it before the airspeed did, the way you know a pressure's dropping a beat before the cuff cycles.`}`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Push the nose down and put it on the numbers anyway. She is not leaving that point.`, s:0, d:{trust:-5,conf:-3,health:-6},
          after:`She pushes. Biscuit drops. Nosewheel and mains together, hard, a bounce, nose up, and she shoves again. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"My airplane."</span> Flat. Kendra's hands, then full power, then sky. Crosswind leg before Kendra hands it back, by the book. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"You have the flight controls."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"I have the flight controls."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"You have the flight controls."</span> Then nothing, from either of them, all the way to downwind.`,
          why:`Pushing the nose over to force a touchdown point produces a nosewheel-first landing and porpoising, which can collapse a nose gear or strike the propeller. The ACS lists any action requiring the evaluator's intervention as unsatisfactory. A go-around was the answer.`},
        {t:`Ask Kendra out loud if she'd rather see a go-around or a long landing.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"You're the pilot in command."</span> That's it. That's all. And by then the numbers are behind them. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Going around."</span> Power, carb heat, flaps in steps. It's a late go-around, a little low and a little untidy, but it's a go-around. The second short field puts the mains down twenty feet past the 35, and Kendra writes for a while.`,
          why:`Asking reflects good instincts about the outcome, but the decision to go around belongs to the PIC and shouldn't wait for permission. The delay made the go-around later and lower than it needed to be.`},
        {t:`Go around. Say it, fly it, and set the short field up again.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:6},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Going around."</span> Full power, carb heat in, nose to the climb attitude, flaps up to twenty, then ten, then up, positive rate. She tells tower. Kendra writes something while they climb. The second approach she flies ready for the bump and holds the numbers still on the windshield, and the mains go down ten feet past the 35 with the stall horn singing, and she stands on the brakes like the POH says to. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Why'd you go around?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"I was going to land long. And I wanted to."</span> Kendra nods. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"The second one is why we do this."</span>`,
          why:`A short field landing that won't meet the point is a go-around, and a go-around flown early and well is good airmanship, not a failure. The ACS tests it as its own task (PA.IV.N). The second approach met the standard: on or within 200 feet past the point, minimum float.`},
        {t:`Hold it off, let it float, and land long. There are ten thousand feet of runway.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`She holds it off. Biscuit floats past the numbers, past the first stripes, a long way past, the stall horn complaining, and finally settles on the mains about five hundred feet beyond the point. A nice landing. In the wrong place. Kendra doesn't say anything on the rollout. On the taxiway she says, <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Where did you touch down?"</span> and Jordan tells her the truth.`,
          why:`The runway's length isn't the standard. PA.IV.F.S10 requires touchdown on the point or within 200 feet beyond it with minimum float. Recognizing early that the approach wouldn't make it, and going around, was the right decision.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s11`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`XII`, title:`Prop's still turning`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const p=(s.picks||{});const failed=(p[9]===0||p[9]===3);return [
        ...(failed?[
          `On downwind Kendra said, carefully, <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"That landing didn't meet the standard for the short field. That's Area IV. I can't pass you today. You can stop here, or we can finish the rest of the test, and you'll keep credit for everything else you pass. Your choice. I need your yes."</span>`,
          `Jordan's throat had gone tight. She did what she did on a call when a family member started screaming: she found the next thing that needed doing. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Let's finish it."</span> They did. A normal landing, inside the 400 feet. A soft field takeoff. Then the taxi back, and the postflight, and Kendra at the round table with the laptop, filling out a form.`,
          `<prop type="doc" title="Notice of Disapproval of Application, FAA Form 8060-5, 03/22/2027">APPLICANT: JORDAN E. REYES<br>CERTIFICATE SOUGHT: PRIVATE PILOT, AIRPLANE SINGLE-ENGINE LAND<br>AREA OF OPERATION NOT MEETING STANDARD: IV. TAKEOFFS, LANDINGS, AND GO-AROUNDS. TASK F, SHORT-FIELD APPROACH AND LANDING.<br>AREAS NOT TESTED: NONE.<br>NUMBER OF PRACTICAL TEST FAILURES: 1.<br>CREDIT FOR AREAS PASSED IF THE REMAINDER IS PASSED BY 05/21/2027 (14 CFR 61.43(f)). PRESENT THIS NOTICE, A NEW APPLICATION, AND AN INSTRUCTOR ENDORSEMENT UNDER 61.49.<br>/s/ K. LOWE, DPE</prop>`,
          `<span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Here's what I saw,"</span> Kendra said, and told her, exactly, without softening it and without making it bigger. Then: <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Everything else today was solid. Some of it was better than that. You'll get one task wrong in an airplane again someday, probably with nobody watching. What you do with the next approach is the part that matters. I had a cancellation this morning for Thursday at nine. It's yours."</span>`,
          `Jordan walked out to the ramp holding the paper by its edges, the way she'd held the knowledge test report. Abby was already there. Abby looked at her face and then at the paper and said, <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Okay. It's eleven. I've got a week I bought for myself. Biscuit at two."</span>`,
          `At two they flew. Point nine on the Hobbs, twelve short field approaches, four of them go-arounds that Abby called and two that Jordan called before Abby could, and the last six inside a hundred feet. On the ramp at 3:10 Abby wrote in green, and that evening Jordan opened a new application in IACRA and Abby signed it as her recommending instructor for the second time.`,
          `<prop type="doc" title="Jordan's logbook, endorsements">03/22/27. I certify that Jordan E. Reyes has received the additional flight training as required by 14 CFR § 61.49. I have determined that they are proficient to pass the Private Pilot Airplane practical test.<br>/s/ 03/22/27 A. Strand 3418862CFI RE 06-30-2027</prop>`,
          `Tuesday she worked a twenty four. Wednesday she slept. Thursday, March 25, at 9:40, Kendra gave her the short field again, with the notice and the new application on the table between them. The mains went down on the 35. Kendra asked for a normal landing after it, and a go-around, and then said, <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Taxi us in."</span>`]
        :[
          `The last landing was a normal one, full stop, inside the 400 feet with room to spare. On the rollout Kendra looked out her side window for a while, at the snow along the edges going gold in the sun, and then she said, <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Taxi us in."</span>`]),
        `Jordan's hands had a little shake in them. She read the after landing checklist out loud anyway, flaps up, carb heat cold, transponder, lights, and turned off at Alpha and told ground where she was going.`,
        `Ahead of her, past the FBO, her mother was standing behind the painted line with Abby and Walt. ${failed?`Lourdes had come back on Thursday too. She'd said on the phone she'd come every time, as many times as it took, and that she wasn't asking.`:`She'd been standing there for two hours and six minutes. Abby would say later that she hadn't sat down once.`} Jordan brought Biscuit around toward the tie down at a walk, feet on the brakes. Halfway through the turn her mother stepped over the painted line. One step. Two. Hands out a little, palms up, the way she used to come at you when you were six and had fallen off something. Looking at Jordan's face through the windshield. Only at that.`,
        `Not at the propeller. Gray blur, idle, about eight feet in front of her.`];},
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep rolling slowly toward the tie down. Abby's right there and will catch her.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:-2,health:-6},
          after:`Abby gets her. Three steps out, a hand on the arm, and the coffee goes all over the concrete. Now Jordan stops. Mixture. The prop winds down and her heart doesn't. Kendra lets the blade come to rest before she speaks. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"Who's responsible for people on the ramp near a turning propeller?"</span> Jordan says it. She already knew.`,
          why:`The pilot in command is responsible for the safety of people near the aircraft. Continuing to taxi and relying on someone else to intervene puts a person near a turning propeller. Stopping and shutting down is the only safe response (PA.XII.A.R4, S2).`},
        {t:`Stop, cut the mixture now, and hold up a flat hand at her until the propeller stops.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:6,conf:5,health:7},
          after:`Brakes. Mixture, cutoff. Checklist later. Her left hand goes flat on the windshield, palm out, the scene hand, the one that keeps a bystander from walking into a car wreck. Eyes on her mother's eyes. Lourdes stops. Engine quits. Blade swings past twice, ticks, and sits. Only then does Jordan run the rest of the shutdown out loud, mags, master, the ELT check she did a beat late and says so. Kendra glances out at Lourdes. Back at Jordan. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"That's postflight,"</span> she says.`,
          why:`With a person approaching a turning propeller, the first action is to stop the airplane and stop the engine, then complete the checklist. The ACS allows memory items first with the checklist reviewed after, and names ramp safety as a postflight risk to manage.`},
        {t:`Stop the airplane and wave her back with an arm out the window, engine idling, until Abby reaches her.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`Brakes. She pops the window and waves her mother back. Her mother waves too. Of course she does; she has never in her life been waved at from an airplane. Abby has her elbow two seconds later and Jordan finally pulls the mixture. Kendra lets the prop stop first. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"You stopped. Good. Next time stop the engine too."</span>`,
          why:`Stopping the airplane was right. But an idling engine still has a turning propeller, and a hand signal means nothing to someone who doesn't know it. Shutting down removes the hazard instead of trying to manage it.`},
        {t:`Turn the airplane away from her with a burst of power, so the propeller's pointed somewhere else.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`She has her hand on the throttle when she sees what's behind the turn: the FBO's glass doors and a line of parked airplanes, and Walt, coming through the gate. A burst of power would put prop blast and grit on all of it. She pulls the throttle back, stops, and cuts the mixture. Her mother stops too, finally, because Abby has her by the arm.`,
          why:`Adding power on a ramp to swing the airplane sends prop blast at people and property behind it and doesn't remove the danger in front. Stop and shut down.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s12`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`II`, title:`Over the panel`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const p=(s.picks||{});const failed=(p[9]===0||p[9]===3);const day=failed?`03/25/2027`:`03/22/2027`;const exp=failed?`07/23/2027`:`07/20/2027`;return [
        `At the round table Kendra printed it out of IACRA, signed it, and slid it across without ceremony, which Jordan would remember as the most ceremonious thing anybody had ever done for her.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Temporary Airman Certificate"><code>UNITED STATES OF AMERICA. DEPARTMENT OF TRANSPORTATION. FEDERAL AVIATION ADMINISTRATION<br>TEMPORARY AIRMAN CERTIFICATE<br><br>THIS CERTIFIES THAT JORDAN E REYES<br>HAS BEEN FOUND TO BE PROPERLY QUALIFIED TO EXERCISE THE PRIVILEGES OF<br>PRIVATE PILOT<br>RATINGS AND LIMITATIONS: AIRPLANE SINGLE ENGINE LAND<br>ENGLISH PROFICIENT<br><br>DATE OF ISSUANCE: ${day}<br>THIS TEMPORARY CERTIFICATE EXPIRES: ${exp} (120 DAYS, 14 CFR 61.17)<br>EXAMINER: K. LOWE, DPE</code></prop>`,
        `Then Kendra did something she hadn't done in ${failed?`two`:`one`} long morning${failed?`s`:``}: she leaned back and put her reading glasses on top of her head. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"You told me Friday you answered the NTSB question as a medic in January. I've had applicants answer every question as somebody else. A pilot they admire. An instructor. A brother."</span> She capped her pen. <span class="said" data-who="Kendra">"You answered mine as you. Go take your niece to Akron. Weigh her first."</span>`,
        `Walt was waiting in the hangar, on Marisol's rolling stool, in his canvas jacket. He'd held up ${s.score>=27?`nine`:`eight`} fingers at the fence as she rolled by on Alpha, and put them away. Now he stood up in stages and handed her an envelope with nothing on the front. Inside was one page from the Hangar Six club agreement, the page with the clause on it, one year from the date of transfer, and in the margin, in two pens and a photo: <b>CONDITION MET. ${day}. W.H. M.T. R.K.</b> Russ had initialed it from Phoenix, by photo, and Marisol had printed the photo and stapled it on, because Marisol.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"It's your share,"</span> Walt said. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"It's been your share ${(G.pushedweather)?`since a night in December when you came back from Larkspur and wrote it down`:`since a night in December when you drove to Pueblo`}. I'm slow, and I like paperwork."</span> He looked at the envelope and not at her. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"In April I offered you book value. I'd like to take that back now, officially. If you ever sell it, sell it to somebody who reads the remarks."</span>`,
        `Abby left on Sunday, March 28. On Saturday afternoon she came by the hangar with her flight bag already packed for good and stood next to Biscuit for a minute with one hand flat on the cowling, like you'd check a horse. Then she gave Jordan a pen. The green one. The one with the chewed cap that had signed every line in Jordan's logbook since April.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Endorsements are green. That was my thing. Remarks are yours, so I don't care what color you use, but you don't have a pen you like, and that's unacceptable."</span> She zipped her jacket. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Send me a picture from Akron. Not of the pie. Of her, in the right seat."</span> At the door she turned around. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"You were the student I was going to log and forget."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"I read the lesson."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Then you know the rest. Don't make it weird."</span> She went. Jordan stood in the hangar holding a pen, which, she understood, was a pep talk.`,
        `Saturday, April 3, 9:40 AM, the tie down. Clear and still and cool, the mountains back where they belonged, the snow gone from the ramp and lying in gray rags along the fences.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Weather, printed at 9:05 AM"><code>METAR KAPA 031453Z 34006KT 10SM CLR 05/M06 A3016 RMK AO2<br><br>METAR KAKO 031455Z AUTO 31008KT 10SM CLR 04/M07 A3011 RMK AO2</code></prop>`,
        `She did the passenger line in her logbook the way she'd once done a run report, every box. Three takeoffs and three landings in the preceding 90 days, same category and class, as the sole manipulator: ${failed?`the checkride's, six with Abby on the afternoon of March 22, and three more on the 25th`:`four on March 22`}. Day, so 61.57(b) didn't come into it. Temporary certificate, photo ID, medical, in the flight bag's inside pocket with a zipper. Weight and balance on the clipboard: Ellie weighed that morning on Marisol's scale, 79 again, in the sneakers, the seat cushion on top, Claire at 174, full tanks. 2,156. CG 43.18. Plan in pencil, KAPA to BEND to Akron, seventy five hundred under the shelf and nine thousand five hundred after it, because the course was 053 magnetic and nine five was odd plus five hundred and more than three thousand feet above the plains.`,
        `${(G.nopersonalmins)?`The card above the mag switch had a new line at the bottom, written Wednesday night at her kitchen table: WITH PASSENGERS: ADD 500 FT AND 5 KNOTS. DAY ONLY TILL I'VE FLOWN 20 HRS AS PIC. BRIEF EVERYONE, EVERY TIME. Hers. All of it, now.`:`The card above the mag switch had a new line at the bottom, written Wednesday night at her kitchen table, in Abby's green pen because it was the one she had: WITH PASSENGERS: ADD 500 FT AND 5 KNOTS. DAY ONLY TILL I'VE FLOWN 20 HRS AS PIC. BRIEF EVERYONE, EVERY TIME. Under the shelf she still stayed three hundred under or lower, Abby's rule, unless she heard the words cleared into the Bravo. Some limits she'd keep because they were good.`}`,
        `Claire was in the back seat, behind the right seat, with her coat in her lap and Danny's old Hangar Six cap on her own head for once. At Christmas she'd made the deal out loud: if you pass, she sits up front, and I sit where I can see her. Now she sat there, belted, and looked out the window at the hangars like they owed her money, and then, when she thought nobody was looking, like they didn't.`,
        `And in the right seat, on Marisol's cushion, which brought her up about four inches, sat Ellie, in a purple fleece, with her chin higher than the glare shield. She could see over the panel. She could see the nose, and the runway past the nose, and the mountains past that. She'd gone completely quiet when she realized it, which for Ellie was a medical event.`,
        `On her head was Danny's headset. The gray one, D.R. scratched into the headband, the cracked ear seal with the black tape. Jordan had cinched it down to the smallest it went and folded a bandanna under the band, and Ellie held it on with both hands anyway, like it might leave. Jordan wore the club loaner. It pinched. Fine.`,
        `Jordan turned around in the left seat and did the briefing, all of it, the ACS list with 91.107 up front. Pilot in command: me. Belt: flap up to open, tab in till it clicks. Harness over the collarbone, not the neck, on the whole time the wheels are turning. The door does a thing at takeoff if the top latch isn't set, so I set it twice and you get to watch me do it. Quiet means quiet when I say so, important stuff included, except traffic, which you call like a clock, two o'clock high, that kind of thing. Nobody walks around the front of the airplane. Not with the prop turning, not with it stopped, not ever. Feet flat on the floor, off the pedals. The yoke in front of you moves when mine moves. Let it.`,
        `Then the emergency part. Claire got there first. Jordan caught it on her face a half second before Ellie let go of the headset and swiveled on the cushion until she was looking dead at her aunt.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"What if your heart stops? Like Daddy's."</span>`];},
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell her that won't happen. Aunt Jordan is fine, and pilots are checked all the time.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"That won't happen, El."</span> A long look. Danny's eyes, exactly. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Daddy said that."</span> Oh. Jordan shuts her mouth, breathes, and gives her the true version instead, with Claire listening hard from the back.`,
          why:`A promise that something can't happen isn't a briefing, and a child who has lost a parent to a sudden cardiac arrest knows it isn't true. Honest, specific information about what's been done and what to do is both kinder and part of the required emergency briefing.`},
        {t:`Tell her the truth: what's been checked, how unlikely it is, and exactly what Ellie would do.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:6,conf:5,health:6},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Probably never. Real answer? After Daddy, all of us got our hearts checked. Mine's normal. Then the FAA doctor listened to it for, like, a weirdly long time, and signed a paper saying so. If I ever feel funny up there, I land, and I tell you why after."</span> She unhooks the hand mic. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"And if I stop talking and you can't wake me up: this button. Hold it. Say help, and say our name, the numbers on the side. Somebody's listening the entire time, El. They answer. Plus Mom's right there."</span> Ellie takes the mic in both hands. Her thumb finds the button and stays off it. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Niner One Echo."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"That's us."</span> She hooks it back up, very carefully, like it's somebody's glasses. Claire, in back, is suddenly very interested in a hangar.`,
          why:`PA.II.B.S2 includes emergency procedures in the passenger briefing. Telling a child the truth about the risk, what's been done about it, and one concrete action she can take respects her question and gives her something real to hold on to.`},
        {t:`Let Claire answer it. She's the nurse, and she's Ellie's mom.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`Jordan glances back. Claire gives the smallest headshake there is. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"Nope. You're the pilot."</span> Fair. Jordan turns around and does it herself: the heart screening in April, the FAA doctor, the button on the mic.`,
          why:`Claire is a natural person to comfort Ellie, but the pilot in command gives the passenger briefing, and the emergency part of it is about what happens in this airplane. That answer has to come from the pilot.`},
        {t:`Skip the emergency part of the briefing today, so the first flight isn't scary for her.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Let's not do that one today, El."</span> Ellie nods. Keeps looking at her. Keeps looking. That question is going to ride to Akron in the right seat, on the cushion, unless somebody answers it. Jordan starts over.`,
          why:`The emergency portion is part of a complete passenger briefing. Leaving it out to spare feelings doesn't remove the worry, it just leaves the passenger without any plan.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills:[
    {after:`f12s4`, title:`Add the column`,
     setup:[`Kendra's laptop says solo 10.2. Jordan's back page says 10.1. The entries are the record. As of February 8, the logbook showed total 61.1, dual 53.2, solo 7.9 (in the version where she drove to Pueblo).`,`Since then: solo February 11, 1.2, and February 23, 1.1. Dual checkride prep with Abby: February 17, 1.3; March 4, 1.2; March 10, 1.1; March 16, 1.0. The test is in March.`,`61.109(a)(4): 3 hours of prep with an instructor within the preceding 2 calendar months from the month of the test. 61.109(a)(5): 10 hours of solo.`],
     fields:[{label:`Solo time from the entries (hours)`,answer:10.2,tol:0.01},{label:`Checkride prep that counts (hours)`,answer:4.6,tol:0.01},{label:`Dual total (hours)`,answer:57.8,tol:0.01},{label:`Total time (hours)`,answer:68,tol:0.01},{label:`Hours over the 40 hour minimum`,answer:28,tol:0.01},{label:`Size of the back page error (hours)`,answer:0.1,tol:0.01}],
     solution:[`Solo: 7.9 + 1.2 + 1.1 = 10.2. The back page had 1.0 for February 23, so it summed to 10.1. Off by 0.1.`,`Prep: 1.3 + 1.2 + 1.1 + 1.0 = 4.6. For a March test the preceding 2 calendar months are January and February, and March itself counts too, so February 17 and all three March flights qualify. All 4.6 counts, and only 3.0 is required.`,`Dual: 53.2 + 4.6 = 57.8. Total: 61.1 + 2.3 solo + 4.6 dual = 68.0. Over the minimum: 68.0 minus 40 = 28.0.`,`When two records disagree, add the entries. The entries are what 61.39(a)(3) has the examiner verifying.`]},
    {after:`f12s5`, title:`Akron, by the numbers`,
     setup:[`Biscuit's weight and balance from chapter 5. Basic empty weight 1,486 lb at +39.5 (moment 58,697 lb-in). Front seats +37.0. Rear seats +73.0. Fuel +48.0, 6 lb per gallon. Baggage +95.0. Maximum weight 2,300 lb.`,`Front: Jordan 162, Ellie 79, seat cushion 3. Rear: Claire 174. Fuel: 40 gallons usable. Baggage: flight bag and a small soft cooler, 12 lb.`,`CG limits: aft +47.3. Forward +35.0 at 1,950 lb or less, +38.5 at 2,300, straight line between (0.01 inch per pound). Round CG to two decimals.`],
     fields:[{label:`Takeoff weight (lb)`,answer:2156,tol:0},{label:`Total moment (lb-in)`,answer:93087,tol:1},{label:`CG (inches)`,answer:43.18,tol:0.01},{label:`Forward limit at this weight (inches)`,answer:37.06,tol:0.01},{label:`Pounds under max gross`,answer:144,tol:0},{label:`Landing weight at Akron after burning 9 gallons (lb)`,answer:2102,tol:0},{label:`Landing CG (inches)`,answer:43.05,tol:0.01}],
     solution:[`Front: 162 + 79 + 3 = 244 lb × 37.0 = 9,028. Rear: 174 × 73.0 = 12,702. Fuel: 40 × 6 = 240 lb × 48.0 = 11,520. Baggage: 12 × 95.0 = 1,140.`,`Weight: 1,486 + 244 + 174 + 240 + 12 = 2,156 lb. Moment: 58,697 + 9,028 + 12,702 + 11,520 + 1,140 = 93,087 lb-in.`,`CG: 93,087 ÷ 2,156 = 43.18 inches. Forward limit: 35.0 + (2,156 minus 1,950) × 0.01 = 35.0 + 2.06 = 37.06. Aft limit 47.3. Inside. Margin: 2,300 minus 2,156 = 144 lb.`,`After 9 gallons: 54 lb off at 48.0, so 2,156 minus 54 = 2,102 lb and 93,087 minus 2,592 = 90,495 lb-in. CG 90,495 ÷ 2,102 = 43.05. The fuel sits aft of the CG, so burning it moves the CG forward, a little.`,`The seat cushion is 3 pounds. It goes on the sheet because it goes in the airplane.`]},
    {after:`f12s9`, title:`The divert`,
     setup:[`Groundspeed check before the diversion: 11.2 NM between checkpoints in 6 minutes 15 seconds.`,`Diversion: from 14 NM past BEND to Colorado Air and Space Port, 14.2 NM at 282 true. Variation 7 degrees east. Estimated groundspeed into the light northwest wind (340 at 6 to 10): 100 knots. Fuel burn 8 gallons per hour.`,`Magnetic course = true course minus east variation. Time = distance ÷ groundspeed. Round minutes to one decimal and fuel to two.`],
     fields:[{label:`Groundspeed on the check (knots)`,answer:107.5,tol:0.2},{label:`Magnetic course to the diversion (degrees)`,answer:275,tol:0},{label:`Time to the diversion airport (minutes)`,answer:8.5,tol:0.1},{label:`Fuel to the diversion airport (gallons)`,answer:1.14,tol:0.02}],
     solution:[`6 minutes 15 seconds is 6.25 minutes, or 6.25 ÷ 60 = 0.1042 hours. 11.2 ÷ 0.1042 = 107.5 knots.`,`Magnetic course: 282 minus 7 = 275. East is least.`,`Time: 14.2 ÷ 100 = 0.142 hours × 60 = 8.5 minutes. Fuel: 0.142 × 8 = 1.14 gallons.`,`In the airplane she said eight or nine minutes and about a gallon. That's what PA.VI.C.S2 asks for: a reasonable estimate, made after the turn.`]},
    {after:`f12s12`, title:`Danny's line`,
     setup:[`The route Danny drew, flown the way Jordan flew south of the shelf in November: KAPA to BEND, 16.4 NM at 079 true; BEND to KAKO, 67.7 NM at 060 true. Variation 7 degrees east.`,`91.159: above 3,000 feet AGL, magnetic course 0 to 179, odd thousands plus 500. The plains east of the Class B sit between about 4,700 and 5,500 feet.`,`Planned average groundspeed 105 knots. Fuel 8 gallons per hour, plus 1.5 gallons for start, taxi, runup, and climb. Day VFR reserve under 91.151: 30 minutes.`],
     fields:[{label:`Total distance (NM)`,answer:84.1,tol:0.01},{label:`Magnetic course, BEND to KAKO (degrees)`,answer:53,tol:0},{label:`Cruising altitude she chose after the shelf (feet MSL)`,answer:9500,tol:0},{label:`Time en route (minutes)`,answer:48.1,tol:0.2},{label:`Fuel with taxi and climb allowance (gallons)`,answer:7.91,tol:0.03},{label:`Fuel including the day reserve (gallons)`,answer:11.91,tol:0.03}],
     solution:[`Distance: 16.4 + 67.7 = 84.1 NM.`,`Magnetic: 060 minus 7 = 053. Eastbound, so odd thousands plus 500. 7,500 is under 3,000 feet AGL out there, so 91.159 doesn't apply to it, and the shelf is above it near Denver. Once past the Class B, 9,500 is about 4,000 to 4,800 feet above the ground and fits the rule.`,`Time: 84.1 ÷ 105 = 0.801 hours × 60 = 48.1 minutes.`,`Fuel: 0.801 × 8 = 6.41, plus 1.5 = 7.91 gallons. Reserve: 0.5 × 8 = 4.0. Total 11.91. Biscuit holds 40 usable. Danny's plan said 84 nm and 9,500 after the shelf. His numbers were right. He just never got to use them.`]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f12s10`, title:`Kendra's standards`, intro:`On Wednesday night, before the checkride, Abby texted nine questions in a row with no punctuation and the subject line FAA-S-ACS-6C. Pick the standard.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Steep turns: bank angle.`, choices:[`45°, plus or minus 5°`,`45°, plus or minus 10°`,`30°, plus or minus 5°`], answer:0, why:`PA.V.A.S3 and S5: a coordinated 360 with approximately 45 degrees of bank, held within 5 degrees, with altitude ±100, airspeed ±10, rollout ±10.`},
      {prompt:`Short field landing: where the wheels go.`, choices:[`Within 400 feet beyond the point`,`On or within 200 feet beyond the point`,`Within 100 feet beyond the point`], answer:1, why:`PA.IV.F.S10: at a proper pitch attitude on or within 200 feet beyond the specified point, with minimum float and no side drift.`},
      {prompt:`Normal landing: where the wheels go.`, choices:[`Within 200 feet beyond the point`,`Within 500 feet beyond the point`,`Within 400 feet beyond the point`], answer:2, why:`PA.IV.B.S10 in the 6C ACS: within 400 feet beyond or on the specified point. The commercial standard is 200, and people mix the two up.`},
      {prompt:`Slow flight: airspeed.`, choices:[`Plus or minus 10 knots`,`Plus 10, minus 0 knots`,`Plus 5, minus 5 knots`], answer:1, why:`PA.VII.A.S5: airspeed +10/−0. Slower than the target puts you closer to the stall than the maneuver intends.`},
      {prompt:`Diversion: altitude and heading.`, choices:[`±200 feet and ±15°`,`±100 feet and ±10°`,`±200 feet and ±20°`], answer:0, why:`PA.VI.C.S3: maintain the selected altitude within 200 feet and heading within 15 degrees.`},
      {prompt:`Straight and level under the hood.`, choices:[`±100 feet, ±10°, ±10 knots`,`±200 feet, ±20°, ±10 knots`,`±200 feet, ±15°, ±5 knots`], answer:1, why:`PA.VIII.A.S2: altitude ±200 feet, heading ±20 degrees, airspeed ±10 knots.`},
      {prompt:`Simulated engine failure: best glide.`, choices:[`±5 knots`,`+10/−5 knots`,`±10 knots`], answer:2, why:`PA.IX.B.S1: establish and maintain the recommended best glide airspeed, ±10 knots.`},
      {prompt:`Snow moves in after the oral. The flight is called off. What does the applicant get?`, choices:[`A Letter of Discontinuance`,`A Notice of Disapproval`,`Nothing until the retest`], answer:0, why:`61.43(e)(2) and the ACS: a test stopped for weather gets a letter of discontinuance listing the areas completed. No new endorsement is needed to resume.`},
      {prompt:`How long credit for the passed areas lasts after a letter or a notice.`, choices:[`30 days`,`90 days`,`60 days`], answer:2, why:`61.43(f): credit for areas passed if the remainder is passed within the 60-day period after the test was discontinued.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`Under 61.39, the instructor's practical test endorsement must certify that the applicant received training within the`, opts:[`60 calendar days before the date of the practical test`,`2 calendar months preceding the month of application`,`90 calendar days before the date of the practical test`], a:1, why:`61.39(a)(6)(i): training within 2 calendar months preceding the month of application, and the applicant is prepared and has shown knowledge of the areas missed on the knowledge test. AC 61-65K A.1 and A.2.`, task:`I.A`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`An applicant's practical test is scheduled for April 14. Which of these instructor prep flights counts toward the 3 hours required by 61.109(a)(4)?`, opts:[`One flown on February 3`,`One flown on January 28`,`One flown on December 30`], a:0, why:`The 3 hours must be within the preceding 2 calendar months from the month of the test. For April, that's February and March (and April itself). January and December don't count.`, task:`I.A`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`An applicant receives a notice of disapproval for one task. Credit for the areas of operation passed is kept if the remainder of the test is passed within`, opts:[`30 days`,`90 days`,`60 days`], a:2, why:`61.43(f)(1): within the 60-day period after the test was discontinued, with the original notice, any required training and endorsement, and a new signed application.`, task:`I.A`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Spaced review, weight and balance. An airplane weighs 2,000 lb with its CG at 40.0 inches. A 40 lb bag is added at 95.0 inches. The new CG is about`, opts:[`40.4 inches`,`41.1 inches`,`42.2 inches`], a:1, why:`Moment: 2,000 × 40.0 = 80,000, plus 40 × 95.0 = 3,800, for 83,800. Weight 2,040. CG = 83,800 ÷ 2,040 = 41.08 inches.`, task:`I.F`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`A temporary airman certificate issued after a practical test is valid for`, opts:[`60 days after the date of issuance`,`90 days, or until the card is mailed`,`up to 120 days`], a:2, why:`61.17(a): a temporary certificate is issued for up to 120 days, and it expires earlier when the permanent certificate is received.`, task:`I.A`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`To carry passengers, a private pilot must have made at least three takeoffs and three landings within the preceding`, opts:[`90 days, in the same category and class`,`60 days, in any category of aircraft`,`calendar year, in the same make and model`], a:0, why:`61.57(a)(1): three takeoffs and three landings within the preceding 90 days as sole manipulator, in the same category, class, and type if a type rating is required.`, task:`I.A`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`A pilot passes the private pilot practical test on March 22, 2027, and has no other qualifying event. Without a flight review, they may act as PIC through`, opts:[`March 22, 2029`,`March 31, 2029`,`March 31, 2028`], a:1, why:`61.56(d): a practical test counts as a flight review. 61.56(c) runs from the beginning of the 24th calendar month before the month of the flight, so it's good through the end of March 2029.`, task:`I.A`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`On a private pilot practical test, a short-field landing must touch down`, opts:[`within 400 feet beyond the specified point`,`anywhere in the first third of the runway`,`on or within 200 feet beyond the point`], a:2, why:`PA.IV.F.S10: at a proper pitch attitude on or within 200 feet beyond the specified point, with minimum float. 400 feet is the normal landing standard.`, task:`IV`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`The private pilot ACS standard for bank angle during steep turns is`, opts:[`45 degrees, plus or minus 5`,`30 degrees, plus or minus 10`,`45 degrees, plus or minus 10`], a:0, why:`PA.V.A: approximately 45 degrees of bank, ±5, with entry altitude ±100 feet, airspeed ±10 knots, and rollout on the entry heading ±10 degrees.`, task:`V`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`A person walks toward an airplane on the ramp while its propeller is still turning. The pilot should first`, opts:[`wave the person away and continue taxiing`,`stop the airplane and shut down the engine`,`turn away from the person using added power`], a:1, why:`PA.XII.A: park considering the safety of nearby persons, and manage passenger and ramp movement. Stopping and shutting down removes the hazard. Added power sends prop blast behind the airplane.`, task:`XII`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`Under 91.107, before takeoff the pilot in command must ensure that each person aboard has been`, opts:[`shown where the fire extinguisher is and how to use it`,`shown the location and use of the ELT switch`,`briefed on how to fasten and unfasten the seatbelt`], a:2, why:`91.107(a)(1): each person must be briefed on how to fasten and unfasten the safety belt and shoulder harness, and (a)(2) notified to fasten them for taxi, takeoff, and landing.`, task:`II`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Spaced review, NTSB. While getting out after a flight, a passenger falls from the step and fractures a wrist. This is`, opts:[`an accident requiring immediate notification`,`not reportable, since the engine was shut down`,`an incident reportable only if the NTSB asks`], a:0, why:`49 CFR 830.2: an accident runs from boarding with intent of flight until all persons have disembarked, and a fracture other than a simple finger, toe, or nose fracture is a serious injury. 830.5 requires immediate notification.`, task:`III.A`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`Spaced review, interception. A military aircraft rocks its wings from slightly above and ahead, then makes a slow level turn. The intercepted pilot should`, opts:[`climb straight ahead and squawk 7600 right away`,`turn away and call the nearest control tower`,`rock the wings and follow the interceptor`], a:2, why:`AIM 5-6-14: this signal means you have been intercepted, follow me. The response is rocking wings and following. Squawk 7700 unless ATC says otherwise, and try to call on guard.`, task:`I.D`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Spaced review, charts. On a sectional, small tick marks around a magenta airport symbol indicate that`, opts:[`the airport has a control tower`,`fuel is available and the field is attended`,`the airport is private and not open to the public`], a:1, why:`The chart legend: ticks around the airport symbol mean fuel is available and the field is tended during normal working hours. Magenta means no control tower; blue means a tower.`, task:`I.E`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Spaced review, navigation. A pilot diverts to an airport 18 NM away with an estimated groundspeed of 90 knots. The time en route is about`, opts:[`12 minutes`,`20 minutes`,`9 minutes`], a:0, why:`Time = distance ÷ groundspeed = 18 ÷ 90 = 0.2 hours, which is 12 minutes.`, task:`VI`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>{
    const p=(s.picks||{});
    const failed=(p[9]===0||p[9]===3);
    const told=(p[11]===1);
    return [
    `Runup on Alpha, near the end of 35R. Mags. Carb heat. Door latches, top one twice. Ellie saw the second one and kept quiet, as briefed, with a face that wasn't quiet at all. Jordan told ground she was ready for departure. Ground told her to monitor tower.`,
    `10:21, cleared for takeoff. Full power. Fifty five, nose up, door shut. And Ellie, breaking a rule she'd been given ninety seconds earlier, whispered, <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"You said it does a thing."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Not if you latch the top."</span>`,
    `Right turn east. Seventy five hundred. She said the shelf thing out loud, for Ellie's benefit this time, and Ellie said it back. SHELF AT EIGHTY, in a robot voice, for some reason. Reservoir off the left wing, the ice on it gone gray and rotten at the edges. BEND. Then northeast, on his line, and twenty seven miles later the Class B was behind them and Jordan let Biscuit climb, slowly, in the cool morning air, to nine thousand five hundred.`,
    `From nine five the plains don't end. Brown, white, brown. Center pivots, dozens, all the same size. Snow hanging on in the north side of every draw. A coal train somewhere off the left wing, on the line toward Akron, that Jordan watched for five minutes and couldn't swear moved. Ellie had both hands clamped on the headset and her nose about an inch from the glare shield.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Is she tired?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Cold air. Thick. Honestly? Best day she's had since October."</span>`,
    `Ellie thought about that. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"She's not out of breath."</span> From the back, a laugh. Claire's real one. Then a cough, to cover it, which fooled nobody.`,
    `Akron came up out of the brown at 11:02, a small grid of streets and a grain elevator and, a mile north, a long gray runway that looked like someone had ruled it on the prairie with a straightedge. No tower. She announced on the common frequency at ten miles and again on the downwind, left traffic for 29, and nobody answered, because nobody else was flying to Akron at eleven on a Saturday in April. Seven thousand and one feet, wind nearly down the centerline. She flew the pattern like there was an examiner in the right seat. There was, kind of. Mains on the numbers. Nose down, gentle. And from the back seat Claire, of all people, said, <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"Huh."</span>`,
    `The FBO had a courtesy car. Old Buick, coffee smell, and on the dash a hula girl wearing a tiny pilot's cap that someone had made out of tape and a bottle cap. The man behind the counter handed over the keys without asking for anything but a first name. <span class="said" data-who="man">"Diner's on Main. Get the cherry if they've got it. Don't tell them I said so, they'll think I'm sweet on somebody."</span>`,
    `It was the kind of diner Jordan had seen a hundred of on calls in small towns east of the city: a counter with eight stools, booths along the windows, a pie case by the register with a handwritten card in front of every pie. Cherry, yes. Also a coconut cream, and Ellie stood in front of the case looking at it for long enough that the waitress came over to see if she was okay. Claire ordered for the table, three slices, two coffees, a chocolate milk with a bendy straw, and when the waitress asked if it was a special occasion Claire said yep, her first flight as a pilot, and my kid's first time riding up front. Then she quit talking for a second and Jordan watched her decide, the way you'd watch somebody decide whether to pull a second dose. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"My husband planned it. He didn't get to come."</span> The waitress didn't make a thing of it. When she came back with the forks there were four, and she laid the extra on the empty side of the booth, across from Ellie, and went off for the pot. Nobody touched that fork the whole hour, and nobody moved it either.`,
    `${failed?`<span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Mom said you had to do one of the landings over."</span> Ellie said it through coconut cream, as a point of information. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Yep. Messed it up Monday. Fixed it Thursday."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Was the lady mad?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Nope. She filled out a form."</span> Ellie found this completely reasonable. Fourth grade has forms too.`:`<span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Was the test lady nice?"</span> Ellie got that out around a mouthful of coconut cream. Jordan said fair, and that fair beat nice. Ellie chewed on it, and the pie, and allowed that Coach was fair. So the test lady was basically Coach, then.`}`,
    `Jordan texted Abby the tie down picture, Ellie up on the cushion with the headset on and her chin clear of the panel by a good two inches. Four minutes, and back it came, from a hotel near the training center. <b>Tall enough. Told you. Now eat the pie.</b>`,
    `Her mother, next. Jordan called from the booth with half a slice still on her plate and Ellie working through the cherries on Claire's.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Ma. I'm in Akron."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lourdes">"I know. Claire sent me a picture of the baby in the front."</span> Jordan could hear the kitchen behind her, the radio going on the counter, the lid on the pozole pot doing its little dance. <span class="said" data-who="Lourdes">"Is it good, the pie?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"It's really good, Ma."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lourdes">"Your brother talked about that pie for three years. He never took me."</span> Then a different kind of quiet, the kind where she was deciding something with a wooden spoon in her hand. <span class="said" data-who="Lourdes">"Ask me again in the summer. When it's warm. I'm not saying yes."</span> And then, before Jordan could answer: <span class="said" data-who="Lourdes">"Call me from Centennial. Not the car."</span>`,
    `Back at the airport Claire took the envelope from Christmas into the FBO to pay for the fuel, the share they'd worked out on the back of it in December, and then went to find the restroom, and then, Jordan suspected, went to stand somewhere for a few minutes by herself. That left the two of them in Biscuit on the ramp, Jordan in the left seat with the door open and her boots on the step, Ellie in the right on her cushion, the headset around her neck now.`,
    `Jordan took the green notebook out of the flight bag. She'd known the last page was there since April. She'd turned to it once, the first week, and seen KAPA and KAKO in pencil, and closed the notebook on her finger, and put a paper clip on it, the way she had with the solo page. Saving it. She took the clip off now.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Danny's logbook, the last page, in pencil">Feb 28. NOT FLOWN YET.<br>KAPA to KAKO. 84 nm. 9,500 after the shelf.<br>Pie at the diner.<br>Take Ellie when she's tall enough to see over the panel.<br>Bring Jordan.<br><br>(Pencil so I can change the date.)</prop>`,
    `She read it to Ellie out loud. The parentheses too. Bring Jordan came out wrong, a little high, and she let it. Ellie didn't move.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"He wanted you to come."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"He wanted me to come."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Well, you came."</span> Ellie checked the cabin, the ramp, the elevator over town, like taking attendance. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"And me. So that's done."</span>`,
    `Jordan sat with that. Then, because Ellie had asked her a hard question that morning and she'd ${told?`answered it straight`:`gotten there in the end`}, she decided to answer the one nobody had asked.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"El. The first day I ever flew Biscuit, after, I came back out here by myself and sat in this seat for ten minutes. Didn't touch anything. Didn't tell anybody why."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Why?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"I didn't know then. I thought I was deciding about the airplane. I wasn't. I'd just found this notebook in the door, with the Tums and his pencil, and it smelled like his car in here, and it was the last place I knew where he was still exactly how he was."</span> She ran her thumb along the cracked green spine. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"I was saying goodbye to your dad. I just didn't know that's what it was. I'd never been at the end before, with him. I'm always at the end, at work. I wasn't there for his."</span>`,
    `Ellie was quiet for a while. Out on the county road a pickup went by. Two fingers up off the wheel, at an airplane. People do that out there.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"What are you doing now? In the ten minutes."</span>`,
    `Jordan looked at the panel. At the card in its IV tape, her capitals, the new line at the bottom${(G.nopersonalmins)?``:` in green`}. At the HI DAD sticker underneath it, nine year old purple, one corner up the way it had been up since she'd first pressed it down in November, and every time since.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Saying hi, I think."</span>`,
    `Ellie reached over and pressed the corner of the sticker flat with her thumb. It stayed down. It was the first time it had stayed down. Neither of them said anything about it, and they sat there for the rest of the ten minutes, not flying anywhere, watching the light go flat and bright across the prairie toward the mountains, which from here were only a faint blue line along the bottom of the western sky.`,
    `When Claire came back Jordan had her own logbook open on her knee, the one she'd bought for twelve dollars in April, the only thing in aviation that is. Every dual line in it had Abby's green ink at the end of it. This line was hers. She uncapped Abby's pen.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Jordan's logbook, 04/03/27">DATE 04/03/27. C172N N4791E. FROM KAPA TO KAKO. TOTAL 0.9. ASEL 0.9. PIC 0.9. DAY LDG 1.<br>REMARKS: First passengers. Ellie, right seat, on the cushion. Claire, back. Ellie could see over the panel. Pie at the diner on Main, cherry and coconut cream. The plan was in pencil, in the door pocket.<br>Brought Jordan.</prop>`,
    `${(s.score>=27)?`She looked at it for a while. It was a good entry. It was hers. She'd read every line of it twice before she wrote the last one, which was a habit she was going to keep.`:`She looked at it for a while. It hadn't been a clean month, start to finish, and she knew which parts she'd want back. It was still the right entry. It was still hers.`}`,
    `Danny's notebook went into the right door pocket, with a pencil. Not the left one, where she'd found it. The left door pops if you forget the top latch, and she wasn't trusting a year of his pencil to a latch. It had ridden in her flight bag for a year, and that was long enough. His could live in his airplane. Hers went home with her.`,
    `Top latch. Twice. Claire in back, Ellie up on the cushion with the gray headset and both hands on it again. Then the briefing, all of it, again, because every flight with people in it gets one. At the emergency part Ellie beat her to it. Hold the button. Say help. Say Niner One Echo.`,
    `Clear prop. Akron traffic, Cessna Niner One Echo, departing runway 29, Akron. Nobody answered. You say it anyway. Then she took her family home.`
    ];
  },
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch12.mp3`, len:`about 70 minutes`}
});
